'\" t
.\" Automatically generated by Pandoc 3.10.2
.\"
.TH "conky" "1" "2026\-09\-04" ""
.SH "نام (NAME)"
conky \- A system monitor for X
.SH "خلاصه دستور (SYNOPSIS)"
\f[B]conky\f[R] [ \f[I]options\f[R] ]
.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
Conky is a system monitor for X originally based on torsmo.
Since its inception, Conky has changed significantly from its
predecessor, while maintaining simplicity and configurability.
Conky can display just about anything, either on your root desktop or in
its own window.
Not only does Conky have many built\-in objects, it can also display
just about any piece of information by using scripts and other external
programs.
.PP
Conky has more than 250 built in objects, including support for a
plethora of OS stats (uname, uptime, CPU usage, mem usage, disk usage,
\(dqtop\(dq like process stats, and network monitoring, just to name a
few), built in IMAP and POP3 support, built in support for many popular
music players (MPD, XMMS2, Audacious), and much much more.
Conky can display this info either as text, or using simple progress
bars and graph widgets, with different fonts and colours.
.PP
We are always looking for help, whether its reporting bugs, writing
patches, or writing docs.
Please use the facilities on GitHub to make bug reports, feature
requests, and submit patches.
.PP
Thanks for your interest in Conky.
.SH COMPILING
For users compiling from source on a binary distro, make sure you have
the X development libraries installed (Unless you configure your build
without X11).
This should be a package along the lines of \(dqlibx11\-dev\(dq or
\(dqxorg\-x11\-dev\(dq for X11 libs, and similar \(dq\-dev\(dq format
for the other libs required (depending on your build options).
You should be able to see which extra packages you need to install by
reading errors that you get from running \(gacmake\(aq.
The easiest way to view the available build options is to run
\(gaccmake\(aq or \(gacmake\-gui\(aq from the source tree, but be
careful when disabling certain features as you may lose desired
functionality.
E.g., with BUILD_MATH disabled you won\(aqt get errors but logarithmic
graphs will be normal graphs and gauges will miss their line.
.PP
Conky has (for some time) been available in the repositories of most
popular distributions.
Here are some installation instructions for a few:
.PP
Gentoo users \-\- Conky is in Gentoo\(aqs Portage...
simply use \(dqemerge app\-admin/conky\(dq for installation.
.PP
Debian, etc.
users \-\- Conky should be in your repositories, and can be installed by
doing \(dqaptitude install conky\(dq.
.PP
Example to compile and run Conky with default components (note that some
build options may differ for your system):
.TP
\f[B]cmake\f[R] \f[B]\-D CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX:string=/usr .\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]make\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]make install\f[R] \f[B]# Optional\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]src/conky\f[R]
.PP
Conky has been tested to be compatible with C99 C and C++0x C++, however
it has not been tested with anything other than gcc, and is not
guaranteed to work with other compilers.
.PP
TIP: Try configuring Conky with \(gaccmake\(aq or \(gacmake\-gui\(aq
instead of just \(gacmake\(aq.
.SH YOU SHOULD KNOW
Conky is generally very good on resources.
That said, the more you try to make Conky do, the more resources it is
going to consume.
.PP
An easy way to force Conky to reload your
\f[I]\(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R]: \(dqkillall \-SIGUSR1
conky\(dq.
Saves you the trouble of having to kill and then restart.
.SH "گزینه‌ها (OPTIONS)"
Command line options override configurations defined in configuration
file.
.TP
\f[B]\-a | \-\-alignment=\f[R] \f[B]ALIGNMENT\f[R]
Text alignment on screen, {top,bottom,middle}_{left,right,middle} or
none.
Can also be abbreviated with first chars of position, ie.
tr for top_right.
Only available with build flag BUILD_X11 enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-b | \-\-double\-buffer\f[R]
Use double buffering (eliminates \(dqflicker\(dq).
Only available with build flag BUILD_X11 enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-c | \-\-config=\f[R] \f[B]FILE\f[R]
Config file to load instead of \f[I]\(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-C | \-\-print\-config\f[R]
Print builtin default config to stdout.
See also the section EXAMPLES for more information.
Only available with build flag BUILD_BUILTIN_CONFIG enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-d | \-\-daemonize\f[R]
Daemonize Conky, aka fork to background.
.TP
\f[B]\-D | \-\-debug\f[R]
Increase debugging output, ie.
\-DD for more debugging.
.TP
\f[B]\-f | \-\-font=\f[R] \f[B]FONT\f[R]
Font to use.
Only available with build flag BUILD_X11 enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-h | \-\-help\f[R]
Prints command line help and exits.
.TP
\f[B]\-i\f[R] \f[B]COUNT\f[R]
Number of times to update Conky (and quit).
.TP
\f[B]\-o | \-\-own\-window\f[R]
Create own window to draw.
Only available with build flag BUILD_X11 enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-p | \-\-pause=\f[R] \f[B]SECONDS\f[R]
Time to pause/wait before actually starting Conky.
.TP
\f[B]\-q | \-\-quiet\f[R]
Run Conky in \(aqquiet mode\(aq (ie.
no output).
.TP
\f[B]\-t | \-\-text=\f[R] \f[B]TEXT\f[R]
Text to render, remember single quotes, like \-t \(aq $uptime \(aq.
.TP
\f[B]\-u | \-\-interval=\f[R] \f[B]SECONDS\f[R]
Update interval.
.TP
\f[B]\-U | \-\-unique\f[R]
Conky won\(cqt start if another Conky process is already running.
Implemented only for Linux, FreeBSD, NetBSD, OpenBSD and Haiku.
.TP
\f[B]\-v | \-V | \-\-version\f[R]
Prints version, build information and general info.
Exits after printing.
.TP
\f[B]\-w | \-\-window\-id=\f[R] \f[B]WIN_ID\f[R]
Window id to draw.
Only available with build flag BUILD_X11 enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-x\f[R] \f[B]X_COORDINATE\f[R]
X position.
.TP
\f[B]\-X | \-\-display=\f[R] \f[B]DISPLAY\f[R]
X11 display to use.
Only available with build flag BUILD_X11 enabled.
.TP
\f[B]\-y\f[R] \f[B]Y_COORDINATE\f[R]
Y position.
.SH CONFIGURATION SETTINGS
This is a list of Conky\(cqs global configuration options.
These belong in the \f[CR]conky.config\f[R] section of your
configuration file, before \f[CR]conky.text\f[R].
.PP
The default configuration file location is
\f[CR]\(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R] or
\f[CR]${sysconfdir}/conky/conky.conf\f[R].
On most systems, \f[CR]$sysconfdir\f[R] is \f[CR]/etc\f[R], and you can
find the sample config file there in \f[CR]/etc/conky/conky.conf\f[R].
.PP
You may want to copy the default config to
\f[CR]\(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R] and modify it there.
User configs can be found at
https://github.com/brndnmtthws/conky/wiki/Configs.
.PP
Optional arguments are generally denoted with parentheses, for example
\f[CR](optional)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]alignment\f[R]
Screen alignment.
May be \f[CR]none\f[R] or one of:
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(22.4n) cw(24.3n) rw(23.3n).
T{
\f[CR]top_left\f[R] (or \f[CR]tl\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]top_middle\f[R] (or \f[CR]tm\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]top_right\f[R] (or \f[CR]tr\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[CR]middle_left\f[R] (or \f[CR]ml\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]middle_middle\f[R] (or \f[CR]mm\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]middle_right\f[R] (or \f[CR]mr\f[R])
T}
T{
\f[CR]bottom_left\f[R] (or \f[CR]bl\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]bottom_middle\f[R] (or \f[CR]bm\f[R])
T}@T{
\f[CR]bottom_right\f[R] (or \f[CR]br\f[R])
T}
.TE
.PP
For \f[CR]panel\f[R] and \f[CR]dock\f[R] windows, it may be more useful
to use one of the following aliases:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
c c.
T{
Alias
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]top\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]top_middle\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]left\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]middle_left\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]right\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]middle_right\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]bottom\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]bottom_middle\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]center\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]middle_middle\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
See also \f[CR]gap_x\f[R] and \f[CR]gap_y\f[R] settings.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]append_file\f[R]
Append the file given as an argument.
.TP
\f[B]background\f[R]
Boolean value.
If true, Conky will fork into the background when started.
.TP
\f[B]border_inner_margin\f[R]
Inner border margin in pixels (the margin between the border and text).
.TP
\f[B]border_outer_margin\f[R]
Outer border margin in pixels (the margin between the border and the
edge of the window).
.TP
\f[B]border_width\f[R]
Border width in pixels.
.TP
\f[B]colorN\f[R]
Predefine a color for use inside \f[CR]conky.text\f[R] segments.
Replace \f[CR]N\f[R] with a digit between 0 and 9, inclusive.
When specifying the color value in hex, omit the leading \f[CR]#\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]console_bar_fill\f[R]
A character to fill the console bars.
.RS
.PP
Default: #
.RE
.TP
\f[B]console_bar_unfill\f[R]
A character to unfill the console bars.
.RS
.PP
Default: .
.RE
.TP
\f[B]console_graph_ticks\f[R]
A comma\-separated list of strings to use as the bars in a graph
rendered to the console or shell.
The first list item is used for the minimum bar height and the last item
is used for the maximum, for example \f[CR]\(dq,_,=,#\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]cpu_avg_samples\f[R]
The number of samples to average for CPU monitoring.
.TP
\f[B]default_bar_height\f[R]
Specify a default height for bars.
.RS
.PP
Default: 6
.RE
.TP
\f[B]default_bar_width\f[R]
Specify a default width for bars.
If not specified, the default value is 0, which causes the bar to expand
to fit the width of your Conky window.
If you set out_to_console = true, the default value will be 10 for the
text version of the bar.
.TP
\f[B]default_color\f[R]
Default text color and border color.
.TP
\f[B]default_gauge_height\f[R]
Specify a default height for gauges.
.RS
.PP
Default: 25
.RE
.TP
\f[B]default_gauge_width\f[R]
Specify a default width for gauges.
.RS
.PP
Default: 40
.RE
.TP
\f[B]default_graph_height\f[R]
Specify a default height for graphs.
.RS
.PP
Default: 25
.RE
.TP
\f[B]default_graph_width\f[R]
Specify a default width for graphs.
If not specified, the default value is 0, which causes the graph to
expand to fit the width of your Conky window.
If you set out_to_console = true, the text version of the graph will
actually have no width and you will need to set a sensible default or
set the height and width of each graph individually.
.TP
\f[B]default_outline_color\f[R]
Default outline color.
.TP
\f[B]default_shade_color\f[R]
Default shading color and border\(cqs shading color.
.TP
\f[B]detect_battery\f[R]
One or more batteries to check in order to use
update_interval_on_battery (comma separated).
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]disable_auto_reload\f[R]
Enable to disable the inotify\-based auto config reload feature.
.TP
\f[B]diskio_avg_samples\f[R]
The number of samples to average for disk I/O monitoring.
.TP
\f[B]display\f[R]
Specify an X display to connect to.
.TP
\f[B]double_buffer\f[R]
Use the Xdbe extension?
(eliminates flicker) It is highly recommended to use own window with
this one so double buffer won\(cqt be so big.
.TP
\f[B]draw_blended\f[R]
Boolean, blend when rendering drawn image?
Some images blend incorrectly breaking alpha with ARBG visuals.
This provides a possible work around by disabling blending.
.RS
.PP
Default: True
.RE
.TP
\f[B]draw_borders\f[R]
Draw borders around text.
.TP
\f[B]draw_graph_borders\f[R]
Draw borders around graphs.
.TP
\f[B]draw_outline\f[R]
Draw outlines.
.TP
\f[B]draw_shades\f[R]
Draw shades.
.TP
\f[B]extra_newline\f[R]
Put an extra newline at the end when writing to stdout, useful for
writing to awesome\(cqs wiboxes.
.TP
\f[B]font\f[R]
Font name in X, xfontsel can be used to get a nice font.
.TP
\f[B]fontN\f[R]
Predefine a font to be used in conky.text segments.
Substitute N by a number between 0 and 9 inclusive.
Use the same format as a font variable.
.TP
\f[B]forced_redraw\f[R]
Boolean value, if true, Conky will redraw everything when you switch the
workspace.
This may cause delays/flickering on some WMs.
.TP
\f[B]format_human_readable\f[R]
If enabled, values which are in bytes will be printed in human readable
format (i.e., KiB, MiB, etc).
If disabled, the number of bytes is printed instead.
.TP
\f[B]gap_x\f[R]
Gap, in pixels, between right or left border of screen, same as passing
\-x at command line, e.g.\ gap_x 10.
For other position related stuff, see \f[CR]alignment\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]gap_y\f[R]
Gap, in pixels, between top or bottom border of screen, same as passing
\-y at command line, e.g.\ gap_y 10.
For other position related stuff, see \f[CR]alignment\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]github_token\f[R]
Specify API token for GitHub notifications.
.RS
.PP
Create an API token at
https://github.com/settings/tokens/new?scopes=notifications&description=conky.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]graph_gradient_mode\f[R] \f[B](rgb|hcl|hsv)\f[R]
Changes the color space used for interpolation.
Arguments are hcl, hsv, and rgb (default).
.RS
.PP
Default: rgb
.RE
.TP
\f[B]hddtemp_host\f[R]
Hostname to connect to for hddtemp objects.
.RS
.PP
Default: 127.0.0.1
.RE
.TP
\f[B]hddtemp_port\f[R]
Port to use for hddtemp connections.
.RS
.PP
Default: 7634
.RE
.TP
\f[B]http_port\f[R]
Port to listen to for HTTP connections.
Default value is 10080, but is blocked by Firefox and Chrome, so you
really want to change it.
.RS
.PP
Default: 10080
.RE
.TP
\f[B]http_refresh\f[R]
When this is set the page generated with out_to_http will automatically
refresh each interval.
.TP
\f[B]if_up_strictness\f[R]
How strict should if_up be when testing an interface for being up?
The value is one of \f[CR]up\f[R] (default), \f[CR]link\f[R] or
\f[CR]address\f[R], to check for the interface being solely up, being up
and having link or being up, having link and an assigned IP address.
.RS
.PP
Default: up
.RE
.TP
\f[B]imap\f[R] \f[B]host user pass [`\-i interval (in seconds)'] [\(lq\-f `folder'\(rq] [`\-p port'] [\(lq\-e `command'\(rq] [`\-r retries']\f[R]
Default global IMAP server.
Default port is 143, default folder is `INBOX', default interval is 5
minutes, and default number of retries before giving up is 5.
If the password is supplied as \(cq*\(cq, you will be prompted to enter
the password when Conky starts.
.TP
\f[B]imlib_cache_flush_interval\f[R]
Interval (in seconds) to flush Imlib2 cache.
.TP
\f[B]imlib_cache_size\f[R]
Imlib2 image cache size, in bytes.
Increase this value if you use $image lots.
Set to 0 to disable the image cache.
.RS
.PP
Default: 4194304
.RE
.TP
\f[B]lowercase\f[R]
Boolean value, if true, text is rendered in lower case.
.TP
\f[B]lua_draw_hook_post\f[R] \f[B]function_name [`function arguments']\f[R]
This function, if defined, will be called by Conky through each
iteration after drawing to the window.
Requires a graphical output (X11 or Wayland).
Takes any number of optional arguments.
Use this hook for drawing things on top of what Conky draws.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
Use \f[CR]conky_surface()\f[R] inside the hook to obtain the current
drawing surface.
.TP
\f[B]lua_draw_hook_pre\f[R] \f[B]function_name [`function arguments']\f[R]
This function, if defined, will be called by Conky through each
iteration before drawing to the window.
Requires a graphical output (X11 or Wayland).
Takes any number of optional arguments.
Use this hook for drawing things on top of what Conky draws.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
Use \f[CR]conky_surface()\f[R] inside the hook to obtain the current
drawing surface.
.TP
\f[B]lua_load\f[R]
List of Lua script paths to load at startup in order to provide Lua
functions for other hooks.
Listed files are loaded (executed) before `lua_startup_hook' and can
(but shouldn\(cqt) run code in global scope.
.RS
.PP
Paths are `;' (semicolon) separated, and can be relative to the config
file path, or absolute.
.PP
The paths were previously \(cq \(cq (space) separated, this
functionality is still supported if `;' isn\(cqt found, but is
deprecated and will be removed in future versions.
Empty paths are skipped so `./example file.lua;' is valid.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]lua_mouse_hook\f[R] \f[B]function_name\f[R]
This function, if defined, will be called by Conky upon receiving mouse
events from X or Wayland.
A table containing event information will be passed to this function as
the first argument.
Use this hook for detecting mouse input and acting on it.
Conky requires that the function declaration has a `conky_' prefix to
prevent accidental calls to the wrong function.
.TP
\f[B]lua_shutdown_hook\f[R] \f[B]function_name [`function arguments']\f[R]
This function, if defined, will be called by Conky at shutdown or when
the configuration is reloaded.
Use this hook to clean up after yourself, such as freeing memory which
has been allocated by external libraries via Lua.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
.TP
\f[B]lua_startup_hook\f[R] \f[B]function_name [`function arguments']\f[R]
This function, if defined, will be called by Conky at startup or when
the configuration is reloaded.
Use this hook to initialize values, or for any run\-once applications.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
.TP
\f[B]mail_spool\f[R]
Mail spool for mail checking.
.TP
\f[B]max_port_monitor_connections\f[R]
Allow each port monitor to track at most this many connections.
.RS
.PP
Default: 256
.RE
.TP
\f[B]max_text_width\f[R] \f[B]integer_number\f[R]
When a line in the output contains `width' chars and the end isn\(cqt
reached, the next char will start on a new line.
If you want to make sure that lines don\(cqt get broken, set `width' to
0.
.TP
\f[B]max_user_text\f[R] \f[B]integer_number\f[R]
Maximum size of user text buffer in bytes, i.e.\ text inside conky.text
section in config file.
.RS
.PP
Default: 16384
.RE
.TP
\f[B]maximum_width\f[R] \f[B]integer_number\f[R]
Maximum width of window.
.TP
\f[B]minimum_height\f[R] \f[B]integer_number\f[R]
Minimum height of the window.
.TP
\f[B]minimum_width\f[R] \f[B]integer_number\f[R]
Minimum width of window.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_host\f[R]
Host of MPD server.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_password\f[R]
MPD server password.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_port\f[R]
Port of MPD server.
.TP
\f[B]music_player_interval\f[R]
Music player thread update interval.
.RS
.PP
Default: update interval
.RE
.TP
\f[B]mysql_db\f[R]
MySQL database to use.
.RS
.PP
Default: mysql
.RE
.TP
\f[B]mysql_host\f[R]
Host of MySQL server
.RS
.PP
Default: localhost
.RE
.TP
\f[B]mysql_password\f[R]
Password of the MySQL user.
Place it between \(dq\-chars.
When this is not set there is no password used.
.TP
\f[B]mysql_port\f[R]
Port of MySQL server.
.TP
\f[B]mysql_user\f[R]
MySQL user name to use when connecting to the server.
Defaults to your username.
.TP
\f[B]net_avg_samples\f[R]
The number of samples to average for net data.
.TP
\f[B]no_buffers\f[R]
Subtract (file system) buffers from used memory.
.TP
\f[B]nvidia_display\f[R]
The display that the nvidia variable will used.
.RS
.PP
Default: $DISPLAY
.RE
.TP
\f[B]out_to_console\f[R]
Print text to stdout.
.TP
\f[B]out_to_http\f[R]
Let conky act as a small http\-server serving its text.
.TP
\f[B]out_to_ncurses\f[R]
Print text in the console, but use ncurses so that conky can print the
text of a new update over the old text.
(In the future this will provide more useful things).
.TP
\f[B]out_to_stderr\f[R]
Print text to stderr.
.TP
\f[B]out_to_wayland\f[R]
Open a Wayland window to display output.
.TP
\f[B]out_to_x\f[R]
When set to no, there will be no output in X (useful when you also use
things like out_to_console).
If you set it to no, make sure that it\(cqs placed before all other
X\-related setting (take the first line of your configfile to be sure).
.RS
.PP
Default: True
.RE
.TP
\f[B]override_utf8_locale\f[R]
Force UTF8.
Requires XFT.
.TP
\f[B]overwrite_file\f[R]
Overwrite the file given as argument.
.TP
\f[B]own_window\f[R]
Boolean, draw conky in own window instead of drawing on root window.
.TP
\f[B]own_window_class\f[R]
Manually set the WM_CLASS name.
.RS
.PP
Default: Conky
.RE
.TP
\f[B]own_window_colour\f[R] \f[B]color\f[R]
Set the background colour of the window.
Takes a hex value with optional alpha (e.g.\ \f[CR]\(aq#fff\(aq\f[R],
\f[CR]\(aq#8000\(aq\f[R], \f[CR]\(aq#8fff\(aq\f[R]), or a valid RGB name
(see \f[CR]/usr/lib/X11/rgb.txt\f[R]).
.RS
.PP
The alpha channel controls background opacity.
For example, \f[CR]\(aq#0000\(aq\f[R] is fully transparent and
\f[CR]\(aq#f000\(aq\f[R] is fully opaque black.
Semi\-transparent backgrounds require a compositor on X11.
.PP
Default: black
.RE
.TP
\f[B]own_window_hints\f[R] \f[B]hint(,hint)\f[R]*
If \f[CR]own_window\f[R] is set, on X11 you may specify comma separated
window manager hints to affect the way Conky displays.
.RS
Following hints are some of the standard WM specification ones:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]above\f[R] indicates that conky should be on top of most windows.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]below\f[R] indicates that conky should be below most windows.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]skip_pager\f[R] indicates that conky should not be included on a
Pager.
\f[B]Implied if\f[R] \f[CR]own_window_type\f[R] is `dock' or `panel'.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]skip_taskbar\f[R] indicates that conky should not be included on a
taskbar.
\f[B]Implied if\f[R] \f[CR]own_window_type\f[R] is `dock' or `panel'.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]sticky\f[R] indicates that the Window Manager SHOULD keep
conky\(cqs position fixed on the screen, even when the virtual desktop
scrolls.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]undecorated\f[R] indicates that conky shouldn\(cqt have any window
decorations (e.g.\ title bar).
\f[B]Implied if\f[R] \f[CR]own_window_type\f[R] is `dock' or `panel'.
Notes:
.IP \(bu 2
Use \f[CR]own_window_type=\(aqdesktop\(aq\f[R] setting as another way to
implement many of these hints implicitly.
.IP \(bu 2
If you use \f[CR]own_window_type=\(aqoverride\(aq\f[R], window manager
hints have no meaning and are ignored.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]own_window_title\f[R]
Allows overriding conky window name.
.RS
.PP
Default: conky ()
.RE
.TP
\f[B]own_window_type\f[R] \f[B](normal|desktop|dock|panel|utility|override)\f[R]
Specifies the type of window conky is displayed as.
Under X11 this requires \f[CR]own_window\f[R] to be set; under Wayland
it selects the wlr\-layer\-shell layer the surface is mounted on (and,
for \f[CR]dock\f[R]/\f[CR]panel\f[R], whether it reserves screen space
via an exclusive zone).
Available types:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]normal\f[R] mode makes conky show as normal window.
This mode can be configured with use of \f[CR]own_window_hints\f[R]
setting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]desktop\f[R] windows are special windows that have no window
decorations, are always visible on the desktop, do not appear in pager
or taskbar, and are sticky across all workspaces.
Many DEs include \f[CR]desktop\f[R] windows for background, icons and
desktop menu, in those cases it might be better to use \f[CR]normal\f[R]
or one of the below options, as those will cover conky when they\(cqre
clicked on.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]dock\f[R] windows are placed on screen edges.
They\(cqre the same as \f[CR]desktop\f[R] in most respects, but render
on top of \f[CR]desktop\f[R] windows and below \f[CR]normal\f[R] ones.
Conky doesn\(cqt define struts for this window type, but some WMs might
still implicitly avoid placing windows on top of it.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]panel\f[R] windows are similar to \f[CR]dock\f[R] windows, but
they also reserve space \f[I]along a workarea edge\f[R] (like taskbars),
preventing maximized windows from overlapping them.
The edge is chosen based on final conky position and size, and workarea
dimensions, to ensure normal windows have most free space available.
For WMs that \(lqcut out\(rq entirely covered screens from reserved
area, the edge will be chosen based on \f[CR]alignment\f[R] setting.
Under Wayland the reserved edge is always derived from
\f[CR]alignment\f[R]; a fully centered (\f[CR]mm\f[R]) alignment has no
edge to reserve against, so use an edge\-aligned value
(e.g.\ \f[CR]tm\f[R], \f[CR]bm\f[R], \f[CR]ml\f[R], \f[CR]mr\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]utility\f[R] windows are persistent utility windows (e.g.\ a
palette or toolbox).
They appear on top of other windows (in the same group), but otherwise
behave much like \f[CR]normal\f[R] windows.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]override\f[R] windows are drawn directly on root window (desktop
background) and are not under the control of the window manager.
These will not work with DEs which draw desktop icons via custom
panels/windows as those will cover conky.
\f[CR]own_window_hints\f[R] are ignored for \f[CR]override\f[R] windows.
Semi\-transparent backgrounds do not work with \f[CR]override\f[R]
windows because compositors typically do not composite
override\-redirect windows.
Full transparency (alpha 0) may work via pseudo\-transparency.
.PP
To make conky mount on root window, set \f[CR]own_window\f[R] to
\f[CR]false\f[R].
.PP
Default: normal
.RE
.TP
\f[B]pad_percents\f[R]
Pad percentages to this many decimals (0 = no padding).
.TP
\f[B]pop3\f[R] \f[B]host user pass [`\-i interval (in seconds)'] [`\-p port'] [\(lq\-e `command'\(rq] [`\-r retries']\f[R]
Default global POP3 server.
Arguments are:
\f[CR]host user pass [\-i interval (in seconds)] [\-p port] [\-e \(aqcommand\(aq] [\-r retries]\f[R].
Default port is 110, default interval is 5 minutes, and default number
of retries before giving up is 5.
If the password is supplied as \(cq*\(cq, you will be prompted to enter
the password when Conky starts.
.TP
\f[B]short_units\f[R]
Shortens units to a single character (kiB\->k, GiB\->G, etc.).
.TP
\f[B]show_graph_range\f[R]
Shows the time range covered by a graph.
.TP
\f[B]show_graph_scale\f[R]
Shows the maximum value in scaled graphs.
.TP
\f[B]stippled_borders\f[R]
Border stippling (dashing) in pixels.
.TP
\f[B]temperature_unit\f[R]
Desired output unit of all objects displaying a temperature.
Parameters are either \f[CR]fahrenheit\f[R] or \f[CR]celsius\f[R].
.RS
.PP
Default: celsius
.RE
.TP
\f[B]templateN\f[R]
Define a template for later use inside conky.text segments.
Substitute N by a digit between 0 and 9, inclusively.
The value of the variable is being inserted into the stuff inside
conky.text at the corresponding position, but before some substitutions
are applied:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(rs\(rsn\f[R] \-> newline
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs\f[R] \-> backslash
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(rs\(rs\f[R] \-> space
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(rs\(rsN\f[R] \-> template argument N (starting from 1)
.RE
.TP
\f[B]text_buffer_size\f[R] \f[B]integer_number\f[R]
Size of the standard text buffer in bytes.
This buffer is used for intermediary text, such as individual lines,
output from $exec vars, and various other variables.
Increasing the size of this buffer can drastically reduce Conky\(cqs
performance, but will allow for more text display per variable.
The size of this buffer cannot be smaller than the default value of 256
bytes.
.RS
.PP
Default: 256
.RE
.TP
\f[B]times_in_seconds\f[R]
If true, variables that output times output a number that represents
seconds.
This doesn\(cqt affect $time, $tztime and $utime.
.TP
\f[B]top_cpu_separate\f[R]
If true, cpu in top will show usage of one processor\(cqs power.
If false, cpu in top will show the usage of all processors\(cq power
combined.
.TP
\f[B]top_name_verbose\f[R]
If true, top name shows the full command line of each process, including
arguments (whenever possible).
Otherwise, only the basename is displayed.
.RS
.PP
Default: false
.RE
.TP
\f[B]top_name_width\f[R]
Width for $top name value in characters.
.RS
.PP
Default: 15
.RE
.TP
\f[B]total_run_times\f[R]
Total number of times for Conky to update before quitting.
Zero makes Conky run forever.
.TP
\f[B]units_spacer\f[R]
String to place between values and units.
.RS
.PP
Default: \(cq\(cq (empty string)
.RE
.TP
\f[B]update_interval\f[R] \f[B]seconds\f[R]
Update interval.
.TP
\f[B]update_interval_on_battery\f[R] \f[B]seconds\f[R]
Update interval when running on battery power.
.TP
\f[B]uppercase\f[R]
Boolean value, if true, text is rendered in upper case.
.TP
\f[B]use_spacer\f[R]
Adds spaces around certain objects to stop them from moving other things
around.
May be \f[CR]none\f[R] (default) or \f[CR]left\f[R] or \f[CR]right\f[R].
The old true/false values are deprecated and default to right/none
respectively.
Note that this only helps if you are using a mono font, such as
Bitstream Vera Sans Mono.
.RS
.PP
Default: none
.RE
.TP
\f[B]use_xft\f[R]
Use Xft (anti\-aliased font and stuff).
.TP
\f[B]xftalpha\f[R]
Alpha of Xft font.
Must be a value at or between 1 and 0.
.TP
\f[B]xinerama_head\f[R]
Specify a Xinerama head.
.SH OBJECTS/VARIABLES
To configure what Conky displays, you must supply some variables in the
\f[CR]conky.text\f[R] section of your configuration.
In this section you will find a listing of the available variables.
Some of them may require build options to be enabled at compile time for
them to work.
.PP
Colors are parsed using \f[CR]XParseColor()\f[R].
A list of named colors may be available in
\f[CR]/usr/share/X11/rgb.txt\f[R].
Colors may also be specified in \f[CR]#rrggbb\f[R] hexadecimal format.
.PP
Some objects may create threads, and sometimes these threads will not be
destroyed until Conky terminates.
There is no way to destroy or clean up threads while Conky is running.
For example, if you use an MPD variable, the MPD thread will keep
running until Conky exits.
Some threaded objects will use one of the parameters as a
\f[CR]key\f[R], so that you only have one relevant thread running (for
example, the $curl, and $rss objects launch one thread per URI).
.PP
Optional arguments are generally denoted with parentheses, for example
\f[CR](optional)\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]acpiacadapter\f[R] \f[B](adapter)\f[R]
ACPI AC adapter state.
On Linux, the adapter option specifies the subfolder of
\f[CR]/sys/class/power_supply\f[R] containing the state information.
Tries \f[CR]AC\f[R] and \f[CR]ADP1\f[R] if no argument is given.
Non\-linux systems ignore it.
.TP
\f[B]acpifan\f[R]
ACPI fan state.
.TP
\f[B]acpitemp\f[R]
ACPI temperature in C.
.TP
\f[B]addr\f[R] \f[B](interface)\f[R]
IP address for an interface, or \(lqNo Address\(rq if no address is
assigned.
.TP
\f[B]addrs\f[R] \f[B](interface)\f[R]
IP addresses for an interface (if one \- works like addr).
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]adt746xcpu\f[R]
CPU temperature from therm_adt746x.
.TP
\f[B]adt746xfan\f[R]
Fan speed from therm_adt746x.
.TP
\f[B]alignc\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Align text to the center.
.TP
\f[B]alignr\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Right\-justify text, with space of N.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd\f[R] \f[B]host port\f[R]
Sets up the connection to apcupsd daemon.
Prints nothing.
.RS
.PP
Default: localhost:3551
.RE
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_cable\f[R]
Prints the UPS connection type.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_charge\f[R]
Current battery capacity in percent.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_lastxfer\f[R]
Reason for last transfer from line to battery.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_linev\f[R]
Nominal input voltage.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_load\f[R]
Current load in percent.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_loadbar\f[R]
Bar showing current load.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_loadgauge\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Gauge that shows current load.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_loadgraph\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
History graph of current load.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_model\f[R]
Prints the model of the UPS.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_name\f[R]
Prints the UPS user\-defined name.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_status\f[R]
Prints current status (on\-line, on\-battery).
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_temp\f[R]
Current internal temperature.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_timeleft\f[R]
Time left to run on battery.
.TP
\f[B]apcupsd_upsmode\f[R]
Prints the UPS mode (e.g.\ standalone).
.TP
\f[B]apm_adapter\f[R]
Display APM AC adapter status.
FreeBSD, OpenBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]apm_battery_life\f[R]
Display APM battery life in percent.
FreeBSD, OpenBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]apm_battery_time\f[R]
Display remaining APM battery life in hh:mm:ss or \(lqunknown\(rq if AC
adapter status is online or charging.
FreeBSD and OpenBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Progress bar.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_bitrate\f[R]
Bitrate of current tune.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_channels\f[R]
Number of audio channels of current tune.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_filename\f[R]
Full path and filename of current tune.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_frequency\f[R]
Sampling frequency of current tune.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_length\f[R]
Total length of current tune as MM:SS.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_length_seconds\f[R]
Total length of current tune in seconds.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_main_volume\f[R]
The current volume reported by Audacious.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_playlist_length\f[R]
Number of tunes in playlist.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_playlist_position\f[R]
Playlist position of current tune.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_position\f[R]
Position of current tune (MM:SS).
.TP
\f[B]audacious_position_seconds\f[R]
Position of current tune in seconds.
.TP
\f[B]audacious_status\f[R]
Player status (Playing/Paused/Stopped/Not running).
.TP
\f[B]audacious_title\f[R] \f[B](max length)\f[R]
Title of current tune with optional maximum length specifier.
.TP
\f[B]battery\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Battery status and remaining percentage capacity of ACPI or APM battery.
An ACPI battery number may be given as an argument.
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]battery_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (num)\f[R]
Battery percentage remaining of ACPI battery in a bar.
ACPI battery number can be given as argument (use \f[CR]all\f[R] to get
the mean percentage remaining for all batteries).
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]battery_percent\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Battery percentage remaining for ACPI battery.
ACPI battery number can be given as argument (use \f[CR]all\f[R] to get
the mean percentage remaining for all batteries).
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]battery_power_draw\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Battery power draw in watts
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]battery_short\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Battery status and remaining percentage capacity of ACPI or APM battery.
An ACPI battery number may be given as an argument.
This mode displays a short status: \f[CR]C\f[R] for charging,
\f[CR]D\f[R] for discharging, \f[CR]F\f[R] for full, \f[CR]N\f[R] for
not present, \f[CR]E\f[R] for empty, and \f[CR]U\f[R] for unknown.
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]battery_status\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Battery status for an ACPI battery.
An ACPI battery number may be given as an argument.
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]battery_time\f[R] \f[B](num)\f[R]
Battery charge/discharge time remaining of ACPI battery.
An ACPI battery number may be given as an argument.
.RS
.PP
Default: BAT0
.RE
.TP
\f[B]blink\f[R] \f[B]text_and_other_conky_vars\f[R]
Let `text_and_other_conky_vars' blink on and off.
.TP
\f[B]buffers\f[R]
Amount of memory buffered.
.TP
\f[B]cached\f[R]
Amount of memory cached.
.TP
\f[B]cat\f[R] \f[B]file\f[R]
Reads a file and displays its contents in Conky.
This is useful if you have an independent process generating output that
you want to include in Conky.
.TP
\f[B]catp\f[R] \f[B]file\f[R]
Reads a file and displays its contents in Conky.
This is useful if you have an independent process generating output that
you want to include in Conky.
This differs from \f[CR]$cat\f[R] in that it parses the contents of the
file, so you can insert things like \f[CR]${color red}hi!${color}\f[R]
in your file and have it correctly parsed by Conky.
.TP
\f[B]cmdline_to_pid\f[R] \f[B]string\f[R]
PID of the first process whose command line contains the given string.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_aaa\f[R]
Print aaa status of cmus (all/artist/album).
.TP
\f[B]cmus_album\f[R]
Prints the album of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_artist\f[R]
Prints the artist of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_curtime\f[R]
Current time of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_date\f[R]
Print the date of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_file\f[R]
Print the file name of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_genre\f[R]
Print the genre name of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_percent\f[R]
Percent of song\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_progress\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
cmus\(cq progress bar.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_random\f[R]
Random status of cmus (on/off).
.TP
\f[B]cmus_repeat\f[R]
Repeat status of cmus (song/all/off).
.TP
\f[B]cmus_state\f[R]
Current state of cmus (playing, paused, stopped etc).
.TP
\f[B]cmus_timeleft\f[R]
Time left of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_title\f[R]
Prints the title of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_totaltime\f[R]
Total length of the current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]cmus_track\f[R]
Print track number of current cmus song.
.TP
\f[B]color\f[R] \f[B](color)\f[R]
Change drawing color to \f[I]color\f[R] which is a name of a color or a
hexcode preceded with #, e.g.\ \f[CR]#0A1B2C\f[R].
If you use ncurses only the following colors are supported: red, green,
yellow, blue, magenta, cyan, black, and white.
.TP
\f[B]colorN\f[R]
Change drawing color to colorN configuration option, where N is a digit
between 0 and 9, inclusively.
.TP
\f[B]combine\f[R] \f[B]var1 var2\f[R]
Places the lines of var2 to the right of the lines of var1 separated by
the chars that are put between var1 and var2.
For example:
\f[CR]${combine ${head /proc/cpuinfo 2} \- ${head /proc/meminfo 1}}\f[R]
gives as output \f[CR]cpuinfo_line1 \- meminfo_line1\f[R] on line 1 and
\f[CR]cpuinfo_line2 \-\f[R] on line 2.
$combine vars can also be nested to place more vars next to each other.
.TP
\f[B]conky_build_arch\f[R]
CPU architecture Conky was built for.
.TP
\f[B]conky_version\f[R]
Conky version.
.TP
\f[B]cpu\f[R] \f[B](cpuN)\f[R]
CPU usage in percents.
For SMP machines, the CPU number can be provided as an argument.
${cpu cpu0} is the total usage, and ${cpu cpuX} (X >= 1) are individual
CPUs.
.TP
\f[B]cpubar\f[R] \f[B](cpuN) (height),(width)\f[R]
Bar that shows CPU usage, height is bar\(cqs height in pixels.
See $cpu for more info on SMP.
.TP
\f[B]cpugauge\f[R] \f[B](cpuN) (height),(width)\f[R]
Elliptical gauge that shows CPU usage, height and width are gauge\(cqs
vertical and horizontal axis respectively.
See $cpu for more info on SMP.
.TP
\f[B]cpugovernor\f[R] \f[B](cpuN)\f[R]
The active CPU scaling governor, defaulting to the first core.
See $cpu for more info on SMP.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]cpugraph\f[R] \f[B](cpuN) (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
CPU usage graph, with optional colours in hex, minus the #.
See $cpu for more info on SMP.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use the \-l
switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]curl\f[R] \f[B]url (interval_in_minutes)\f[R]
Download data from URI using Curl at the specified interval.
The interval may be a positive floating point value (0 is allowed),
otherwise defaults to 15 minutes.
Most useful when used in conjunction with Lua and the Lua API.
This object is threaded, and once a thread is created it can\(cqt be
explicitly destroyed.
One thread will run for each URI specified.
You can use any protocol that Curl supports.
.TP
\f[B]desktop\f[R]
Number of the desktop on which conky is running or the message \(lqNot
running in X\(rq if this is the case.
.TP
\f[B]desktop_name\f[R]
Name of the desktop on which conky is running or the message \(lqNot
running in X\(rq if this is the case.
.TP
\f[B]desktop_number\f[R]
Number of desktops or the message \(lqNot running in X\(rq if this is
the case.
.TP
\f[B]disk_protect\f[R] \f[B]device\f[R]
Disk protection status, if supported (needs kernel\-patch).
Prints either \(lqfrozen\(rq or \(lqfree\(rq (note the padding).
.TP
\f[B]diskio\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Displays current disk IO.
Device is optional, and takes the form of sda for /dev/sda.
A block device label can be specified with label:foo and a block device
partuuid can be specified with partuuid:40000000\-01.
.TP
\f[B]diskio_read\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Displays current disk IO for reads.
Device as in diskio.
.TP
\f[B]diskio_write\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Displays current disk IO for writes.
Device as in diskio.
.TP
\f[B]diskiograph\f[R] \f[B](device) (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Disk IO graph, colours defined in hex, minus the #.
If scale is non\-zero, it becomes the scale for the graph.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use \-l switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]diskiograph_read\f[R] \f[B](device) (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Disk IO graph for reads, colours defined in hex, minus the
.RS
.IP "1." 3
If scale is non\-zero, it becomes the scale for the graph.
Device as in diskio.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use \-l switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]diskiograph_write\f[R] \f[B](device) (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Disk IO graph for writes, colours defined in hex, minus the
.RS
.IP "1." 3
If scale is non\-zero, it becomes the scale for the graph.
Device as in diskio.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use \-l switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]distribution\f[R]
Name of the Linux distribution.
\f[CR]NAME\f[R] is read from the \f[CR]/etc/os\-release\f[R] file with
fallback on \f[CR]/proc/version\f[R] content.
Create an issue if reported information is wrong.
.TP
\f[B]downspeed\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Download speed in suitable IEC units.
.TP
\f[B]downspeedf\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Download speed in KiB with one decimal.
.TP
\f[B]downspeedgraph\f[R] \f[B](netdev) (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m)\f[R]
Download speed graph, colours defined in hex, minus the #.
If scale is non\-zero, it defines the maximum value of the graph (in
bytes per second).
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use \-l switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]draft_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails marked as draft in the specified mailbox or mail spool
if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]else\f[R]
Text to show if any of the above are not true.
.TP
\f[B]endif\f[R]
Ends an $if block.
.TP
\f[B]entropy_avail\f[R]
Current entropy available for crypto freaks.
.TP
\f[B]entropy_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Normalized bar of available entropy for crypto freaks.
.TP
\f[B]entropy_perc\f[R]
Percentage of entropy available in comparison to the poolsize.
.TP
\f[B]entropy_poolsize\f[R]
Total size of system entropy pool for crypto freaks.
.TP
\f[B]eval\f[R] \f[B]string\f[R]
Evaluates given string according to the rules of conky.text
interpretation, i.e.\ parsing any contained text object specifications
into their output, any occurring
`$′\f[I]i\f[R]\f[I]n\f[R]\f[I]t\f[R]\f[I]o\f[R]\f[I]a\f[R]\f[I]s\f[R]\f[I]i\f[R]\f[I]n\f[R]\f[I]g\f[R]\f[I]l\f[R]\f[I]e\f[R]^′^'
and so on.
The output is then being parsed again.
.TP
\f[B]exec\f[R] \f[B]command\f[R]
Executes a shell command and displays the output in conky.
Warning: this takes a lot more resources than other variables.
I\(cqd recommend coding wanted behaviour in C/C++ and posting a patch.
.TP
\f[B]execbar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) command\f[R]
Same as exec, except if the first value returned is a value between
0\-100, it will use that number to draw a horizontal bar.
The height and width parameters are optional, and default to the
default_bar_height and default_bar_width config settings, respectively.
.TP
\f[B]execgauge\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) command\f[R]
Same as exec, except if the first value returned is a value between
0\-100, it will use that number to draw a round gauge (much like a
vehicle speedometer).
The height and width parameters are optional, and default to the
default_gauge_height and default_gauge_width config settings,
respectively.
.TP
\f[B]execgraph\f[R] \f[B]command (height),(width) (gradient color 1) (gradient color 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Draws a horizontally scrolling graph with values from 0\-100 plotted on
the vertical axis.
All parameters following the command are optional.
Gradient colors can be specified as hexadecimal values with no 0x or #
prefix.
Use the \-t switch to enable a temperature gradient, so that small
values are \(lqcold\(rq with color 1 and large values are \(lqhot\(rq
with color 2.
Without the \-t switch, the colors produce a horizontal gradient
spanning the width of the graph.
The scale parameter defines the maximum value of the graph.
Use the \-l switch to enable a logarithmic scale, which helps to see
small values.
The default size for graphs can be controlled via the
default_graph_height and default_graph_width config settings.
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.RS
.PP
If you need to execute a command with spaces, you have a couple options:
.IP "1." 3
wrap your command in double\-quotes, or
.IP "2." 3
put your command into a separate file, such as \(ti/bin/myscript.sh, and
use that as your execgraph command.
.PP
Remember to make your script executable!
.PP
In the following example, we set up execgraph to display seconds (0\-59)
on a graph that is 50px high and 200px wide, using a temperature
gradient with colors ranging from red for small values (FF0000) to
yellow for large values (FFFF00).
We set the scale to 60.
.IP
.EX
${execgraph \(ti/seconds.sh 50,200 FF0000 FFFF00 60 \-t}
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]execi\f[R] \f[B]interval command\f[R]
Same as exec, but with a specific interval in seconds.
The interval can\(cqt be less than the update_interval in your
configuration.
See also $texeci.
.TP
\f[B]execibar\f[R] \f[B]interval (height),(width) command\f[R]
Same as execbar, but with an interval.
.TP
\f[B]execigauge\f[R] \f[B]interval (height),(width) command\f[R]
Same as execgauge, but with an interval.
.TP
\f[B]execigraph\f[R] \f[B]interval command (height),(width) (gradient color 1) (gradient color 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Same as execgraph, but with an interval.
.TP
\f[B]execp\f[R] \f[B]command\f[R]
Executes a shell command and displays the output in conky.
Warning: this takes a lot more resources than other variables.
I\(cqd recommend coding wanted behaviour in C/C++ and posting a patch.
This differs from
$exec in that it parses the output of the command, so you
can insert things like \(ga${color red}hi!${color}\(ga in your script
and have it correctly parsed by Conky.
Caveats: Conky parses and evaluates the output of $execp every time
Conky loops, and then destroys all the objects.
If you try to use anything like $execi within an $execp statement, it
will functionally run at the same interval that the $execp statement
runs, as it is created and destroyed at every interval.
.TP
\f[B]execpi\f[R] \f[B]interval command\f[R]
Same as execp, but with an interval.
Note that the output from the $execpi command is still parsed and
evaluated at every interval.
.TP
\f[B]flagged_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails marked as flagged in the specified mailbox or mail spool
if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]font\f[R] \f[B](font)\f[R]
Specify a different font.
This new font will apply to the current line and everything following.
You can use a $font with no arguments to change back to the default font
(much like with $color).
.TP
\f[B]fontN\f[R]
Change font to fontN configuration option, where N is a digit between 0
and 9, inclusively.
.TP
\f[B]format_time\f[R] \f[B]seconds format\f[R]
Format time given in seconds.
This var only works when the times_in_seconds configuration setting is
on.
Format is a string that should start and end with a double quote
\f[CR]\(dq\f[R] character.
The quote characters are not part of the output, ,,,(,) and \(rs are
replaced by weeks,days,hours,minutes,seconds,(,) and .
If you leave out a unit, it\(cqs value will be expressed in the highest
unit lower than the one left out.
Text between ()\-chars will not be visible if a replaced unit in this
text is 0.
If seconds is a decimal number then you can see the numbers behind the
point by using followed by a number that specifies the amount of digits
behind the point that you want to see (maximum 9).
You can also place a `x' behind so you have all digits behind the point
and no trailing zero\(cqs.
(also maximum 9).
.TP
\f[B]forwarded_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails marked as forwarded in the specified mailbox or mail
spool if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]free_bufcache\f[R]
Amount of memory cached or buffered, as reported by free.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]free_cached\f[R]
Amount of memory cached, as reported by free.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]freq\f[R] \f[B](n)\f[R]
Returns CPU #n\(cqs frequency in MHz.
CPUs are counted from 1.
.RS
.PP
Default: 1
.RE
.TP
\f[B]freq2\f[R] \f[B](n)\f[R]
Returns CPU #n\(cqs clock speed from assembly in MHz.
CPUs are counted from 1.
.RS
.PP
Default: 1
.RE
.TP
\f[B]freq_g\f[R] \f[B](n)\f[R]
Returns CPU #n\(cqs frequency in GHz.
CPUs are counted from 1.
.RS
.PP
Default: 1
.RE
.TP
\f[B]fs_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) fs\f[R]
Bar that shows how much space is used on a file system.
height is the height in pixels.
fs is any file on that file system.
.TP
\f[B]fs_bar_free\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) fs\f[R]
Bar that shows how much space is free on a file system.
height is the height in pixels.
fs is any file on that file system.
.TP
\f[B]fs_free\f[R] \f[B](fs)\f[R]
Free space on a file system available for users.
.TP
\f[B]fs_free_perc\f[R] \f[B](fs)\f[R]
Free percentage of space on a file system available for users.
.TP
\f[B]fs_size\f[R] \f[B](fs)\f[R]
File system size.
.TP
\f[B]fs_type\f[R] \f[B](fs)\f[R]
File system type.
.TP
\f[B]fs_used\f[R] \f[B](fs)\f[R]
File system used space.
.TP
\f[B]fs_used_perc\f[R] \f[B](fs)\f[R]
Percent of file system used space.
.TP
\f[B]gid_name\f[R] \f[B]gid\f[R]
Name of group with this gid.
.TP
\f[B]github_notifications\f[R]
Number of GitHub notifications.
.TP
\f[B]goto\f[R] \f[B]x\f[R]
The next element will be printed at position `x'.
.TP
\f[B]gw_iface\f[R]
Displays the default route\(cqs interface or
\(lqmultiple\(rq/\(lqnone\(rq accordingly.
.TP
\f[B]gw_ip\f[R]
Displays the default gateway\(cqs IP or \(lqmultiple\(rq/\(lqnone\(rq
accordingly.
.TP
\f[B]hddtemp\f[R] \f[B](dev)\f[R]
Displays temperature of a selected hard disk drive as reported by the
hddtemp daemon.
Use hddtemp_host and hddtemp_port to specify a host and port for all
hddtemp objects.
If no dev parameter is given, the first disk returned by the hddtemp
daemon is used.
.TP
\f[B]head\f[R] \f[B]logfile lines (next_check)\f[R]
Displays first N lines of supplied text file.
The file is checked every `next_check' update.
If next_check is not supplied, Conky defaults to 2.
Max of 30 lines can be displayed, or until the text buffer is filled.
.TP
\f[B]hr\f[R] \f[B](height) (width)\f[R]
Horizontal line, height is the height in pixels.
width can be used to set the width, in pixels, of the horizontal line.
.TP
\f[B]hwmon\f[R] \f[B](dev) type n (factor offset)\f[R]
Hwmon sensor from sysfs (Linux 2.6).
Parameter dev can be:
.RS
.IP "1." 3
Number.
e.g \f[CR]1\f[R] means hwmon1.
.IP "2." 3
Module name.
e.g.\ \f[CR]k10temp\f[R] means the first hwmon device whose module name
is \f[CR]k10temp\f[R].
.IP "3." 3
Omitted.
Then the first hwmon device (hwmon0) will be used.
.PP
Parameter type is either \f[CR]in\f[R] or \f[CR]vol\f[R] meaning
voltage; \f[CR]fan\f[R] meaning fan; \f[CR]temp\f[R] meaning
temperature.
Parameter n is number of the sensor.
See \f[CR]/sys/class/hwmon/\f[R] on your local computer.
The optional arguments \f[CR]factor\f[R] and \f[CR]offset\f[R] allow
precalculation of the raw input, which is being modified as follows:
\f[CR]input = input * factor + offset\f[R].
Note that they have to be given as decimal values (i.e.\ contain at
least one decimal place).
.RE
.TP
\f[B]hwmonbar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (dev) type n (factor offset)\f[R]
Same as hwmon, but displays the sensor reading as a horizontal bar.
Use factor and offset to scale the value reported by hwmon into the
0\-100 range.
Temperature values are already converted from millidegrees.
Height and width default to default_bar_height and default_bar_width
respectively.
.TP
\f[B]i2c\f[R] \f[B](dev) type n (factor offset)\f[R]
I2C sensor from sysfs (Linux 2.6).
Parameter dev may be omitted if you have only one I2C device.
Parameter type is either \f[CR]in\f[R] or \f[CR]vol\f[R] meaning
voltage; \f[CR]fan\f[R] meaning fan; \f[CR]temp\f[R] meaning
temperature.
Parameter n is number of the sensor.
See \f[CR]/sys/bus/i2c/devices/\f[R] on your local computer.
The optional arguments \f[CR]factor\f[R] and \f[CR]offset\f[R] allow
precalculation of the raw input, which is being modified as follows:
\f[CR]input = input * factor + offset\f[R].
Note that they have to be given as decimal values (i.e.\ contain at
least one decimal place).
.TP
\f[B]i2cbar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (dev) type n (factor offset)\f[R]
Same as i2c, but displays the sensor reading as a horizontal bar.
Use factor and offset to scale the value reported by i2c into the 0\-100
range.
Temperature values are already converted from millidegrees.
Height and width default to default_bar_height and default_bar_width
respectively.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_ac_status\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays whether
ac power is on, as listed in \f[CR]/proc/i8k\f[R] (translated to
human\-readable).
Beware that this is by default not enabled by i8k itself.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_bios\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the bios
version as listed in /proc/i8k.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_buttons_status\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the
volume buttons status as listed in /proc/i8k.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_cpu_temp\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the cpu
temperature in Celsius, as reported by /proc/i8k.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_left_fan_rpm\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the left
fan\(cqs rate of rotation, in revolutions per minute as listed in
/proc/i8k.
Beware, some laptops i8k reports these fans in reverse order.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_left_fan_status\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the left
fan status as listed in /proc/i8k (translated to human\-readable).
Beware, some laptops i8k reports these fans in reverse order.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_right_fan_rpm\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the
right fan\(cqs rate of rotation, in revolutions per minute as listed in
/proc/i8k.
Beware, some laptops i8k reports these fans in reverse order.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_right_fan_status\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the
right fan status as listed in /proc/i8k (translated to human\-readable).
Beware, some laptops i8k reports these fans in reverse order.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_serial\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays your
laptop serial number as listed in /proc/i8k.
.TP
\f[B]i8k_version\f[R]
If running the i8k kernel driver for Inspiron laptops, displays the
version formatting of /proc/i8k.
.TP
\f[B]ibm_brightness\f[R]
If running the IBM ACPI, displays the brigtness of the laptops\(cqs LCD
(0\-7).
.TP
\f[B]ibm_fan\f[R]
If running the IBM ACPI, displays the fan speed.
.TP
\f[B]ibm_temps\f[R] \f[B]N\f[R]
If running the IBM ACPI, displays the temperatures from the IBM
temperature sensors (N=0..7) Sensor 0 is on the CPU, 3 is on the GPU.
.TP
\f[B]ibm_thinklight\f[R]
If running the IBM ACPI, displays the status of your ThinkLight™.
Value is either `on', `off' or `unknown'.
.TP
\f[B]ibm_volume\f[R]
If running the IBM ACPI, displays the \(lqmaster\(rq volume, controlled
by the volume keys (0\-14).
.TP
\f[B]ical\f[R] \f[B]number file\f[R]
Shows title of event number `number' in the ical (RFC 5545) file `file'.
The events are first ordered by starting time, events that started in
the past are ignored.
The events that are shown are the VEVENTS, the title that is shown is
the SUMMARY and the starting time used for sorting is DTSTART.
.TP
\f[B]iconv_start\f[R] \f[B]codeset_from codeset_to\f[R]
Convert text from one codeset to another using GNU iconv.
Needs to be stopped with iconv_stop.
.TP
\f[B]iconv_stop\f[R]
Stop iconv codeset conversion.
.TP
\f[B]if_empty\f[R] \f[B](var)\f[R]
if conky variable VAR is empty, display everything between $if_empty and
the matching $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_existing\f[R] \f[B]file (string)\f[R]
if FILE exists, display everything between if_existing and the matching
$endif.
The optional second parameter checks for FILE containing the specified
string and prints everything between $if_existing and the matching
$endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_gw\f[R]
if there is at least one default gateway, display everything between
$if_gw and the matching $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_match\f[R] \f[B]expression\f[R]
Evaluates the given boolean expression, printing everything between
$if_match and the matching $endif depending on whether the evaluation
returns true or not.
Valid expressions consist of a left side, an operator and a right side.
Left and right sides are being parsed for contained text objects before
evaluation.
.RS
.PP
Recognised left and right side types are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]double\f[R]: Argument consists of only digits and a single dot.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]long\f[R]: Argument consists of only digits.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]string\f[R]: Argument is enclosed in quotation marks
(\f[CR]\(dq\f[R]).
.PP
Valid operands are:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<\f[R] or \f[CR]>\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]<=\f[R] or \f[CR]>=\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]==\f[R] or \f[CR]!=\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]if_mixer_mute\f[R] \f[B](mixer)\f[R]
If mixer exists, display everything between $if_mixer_mute and the
matching $endif.
If no mixer is specified, \(lqVol\(rq is used.
.TP
\f[B]if_mounted\f[R] \f[B](mountpoint)\f[R]
if MOUNTPOINT is mounted, display everything between $if_mounted and the
matching $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_mpd_playing\f[R]
if mpd is playing or paused, display everything between $if_mpd_playing
and the matching $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_pa_sink_muted\f[R]
If Pulseaudio\(cqs default sink is muted, display everything between
$if_pa_sink_muted and the corresponding $else or $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_pa_source_muted\f[R]
If Pulseaudio\(cqs default source (e.g.\ your microphone) is muted,
display everything between $if_pa_source_muted and the corresponding
$else or $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_pa_source_running\f[R]
If Pulseaudio\(cqs default source is running (e.g.\ a program is
accessing your microphone), display everything between
$if_pa_source_running and the corresponding $else or $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_running\f[R] \f[B](process)\f[R]
If PROCESS is running, display everything between \f[CR]$if_running\f[R]
and the corresponding \f[CR]$else\f[R] or \f[CR]$endif\f[R].
Note that PROCESS may be either a full command line with arguments
(without the directory prefix), or simply the name of an executable.
For example, either of the following will be true if there is a running
process with the command line \f[CR]/usr/bin/conky \-u 5\f[R]:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]${if_running conky \-u 5}\f[R] or
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]${if_running conky}\f[R]
.PP
It is important not to include trailing spaces.
For example, \f[CR]${if_running conky }\f[R] will be false.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]if_smapi_bat_installed\f[R] \f[B](INDEX)\f[R]
when using smapi, if the battery with index INDEX is installed, display
everything between $if_smapi_bat_installed and the matching $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_up\f[R] \f[B](interface)\f[R]
if INTERFACE exists and is up, display everything between $if_up and the
matching $endif.
.TP
\f[B]if_updatenr\f[R] \f[B](updatenr)\f[R]
If it\(cqs the UPDATENR\-th time that conky updates, display everything
between $if_updatenr and the matching $endif.
The counter resets when the highest UPDATENR is reached.
.RS
.PP
Example:
\f[CR]{$if_updatenr 1}foo$endif{$if_updatenr 2}bar$endif{$if_updatenr 4}$endif\f[R]
shows foo 25% of the time followed by bar 25% of the time followed by
nothing the other half of the time.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]if_xmms2_connected\f[R]
Display everything between $if_xmms2_connected and the matching $endif
if xmms2 is running.
.TP
\f[B]iface\f[R] \f[B](number)\f[R]
Display interface names starting from 1, eg ${iface 1}.
.TP
\f[B]image\f[R] \f[B] (\-p x,y) (\-s WxH) (\-n) (\-f interval)\f[R]
Renders an image from the path specified using Imlib2.
Takes 4 optional arguments: a position, a size, a no\-cache switch, and
a cache flush interval.
Changing the x,y position will move the position of the image, and
changing the WxH will scale the image.
If you specify the no\-cache flag (\-n), the image will not be cached.
Alternately, you can specify the \-f int switch to specify a cache flush
interval for a particular image.
Example: ${image /home/brenden/cheeseburger.jpg \-p 20,20 \-s 200x200}
will render `cheeseburger.jpg' at (20,20) scaled to 200x200 pixels.
Conky does not make any attempt to adjust the position (or any other
formatting) of images, they are just rendered as per the arguments
passed.
The only reason $image is part of the conky.text section, is to allow
for runtime modifications, through $execp $lua_parse, or some other
method.
.TP
\f[B]imap_messages\f[R] \f[B](args)\f[R]
Displays the number of messages in your global IMAP inbox by default.
You can define individual IMAP inboxes separately by passing arguments
to this object.
Arguments are: \(lqhost user pass [\-i interval (in seconds)] [\-f
`folder'] [\-p port] [\-e `command'] [\-r retries]\(rq.
Default port is 143, default folder is `INBOX', default interval is 5
minutes, and default number of retries before giving up is 5.
If the password is supplied as \(cq*\(cq, you will be prompted to enter
the password when Conky starts.
.TP
\f[B]imap_unseen\f[R] \f[B](args)\f[R]
Displays the number of unseen messages in your global IMAP inbox by
default.
You can define individual IMAP inboxes separately by passing arguments
to this object.
Arguments are: \(lqhost user pass [\-i interval (in seconds)] [\-f
`folder'] [\-p port] [\-e `command'] [\-r retries]\(rq.
Default port is 143, default folder is `INBOX', default interval is 5
minutes, and default number of retries before giving up is 5.
If the password is supplied as \(cq*\(cq, you will be prompted to enter
the password when Conky starts.
.TP
\f[B]intel_backlight\f[R]
Display the brightness of your Intel backlight in percent.
.TP
\f[B]ioscheduler\f[R] \f[B]disk\f[R]
Prints the current ioscheduler used for the given disk name
(i.e.\ e.g.\ \(lqhda\(rq or \(lqsdb\(rq).
.TP
\f[B]irc\f[R] \f[B]server(:port) #channel (max_msg_lines)\f[R]
Shows everything that\(cqs being told in #channel on IRCserver `server'.
TCP\-port 6667 is used for the connection unless `port' is specified.
Shows everything since the last time or the last `max_msg_lines' entries
if specified.
.TP
\f[B]journal\f[R] \f[B](lines) (type)\f[R]
Displays last N lines of the systemd journal.
N defaults to 1 if omitted.
Use 0 to display all available lines.
Negative values are clamped to 1.
The optional type filters which journal is read: `system' shows only the
system journal, `user' shows only the current user\(cqs journal,
`runtime' shows only the runtime journal, `local' shows all local
journals (the default, same as omitting the type).
Output is limited by the text buffer size.
.TP
\f[B]kernel\f[R]
Kernel version.
.TP
\f[B]key_caps_lock\f[R]
An indicator for Capital Lock key.
.TP
\f[B]key_num_lock\f[R]
An indicator for Number Lock key.
.TP
\f[B]key_scroll_lock\f[R]
An indicator for Scrolling Lock key.
.TP
\f[B]keyboard_layout\f[R]
Display keyboard layout.
.TP
\f[B]laptop_mode\f[R]
The value of \f[CR]/proc/sys/vm/laptop_mode\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]legacymem\f[R]
Amount of memory used, calculated the same way as in the \f[CR]free\f[R]
program.
.TP
\f[B]lines\f[R] \f[B]textfile\f[R]
Displays the number of lines in the given file.
.TP
\f[B]loadavg\f[R] \f[B](1|2|3)\f[R]
System load average, 1 is for past 1 minute, 2 for past 5 minutes and 3
for past 15 minutes.
Without argument, prints all three values separated by whitespace.
.TP
\f[B]loadgraph\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Load1 average graph, similar to xload, with optional colours in hex,
minus the #.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use the \-l
switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]lowercase\f[R] \f[B]text\f[R]
Converts all letters into lowercase.
.TP
\f[B]lua\f[R] \f[B]function_name (function parameters)\f[R]
Executes a Lua function with given parameters, then prints the returned
string.
See also `lua_load' on how to load scripts.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
.TP
\f[B]lua_bar\f[R] \f[B](height,width) function_name (function parameters)\f[R]
Executes a Lua function with given parameters and draws a bar.
Expects result value to be an integer between 0 and 100.
See also `lua_load' on how to load scripts.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
.TP
\f[B]lua_gauge\f[R] \f[B](height,width) function_name (function parameters)\f[R]
Executes a Lua function with given parameters and draws a gauge.
Expects result value to be an integer between 0 and 100.
See also `lua_load' on how to load scripts.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
.TP
\f[B]lua_graph\f[R] \f[B]function_name (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Executes a Lua function with and draws a graph.
Expects result value to be any number, and by default will scale to show
the full range.
See also `lua_load' on how to load scripts.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you put you place `conky_' in front
of it yourself.
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]lua_parse\f[R] \f[B]function_name (function parameters)\f[R]
Executes a Lua function with given parameters as per $lua, then parses
and prints the result value as per the syntax for the conky.text
section.
See also `lua_load' on how to load scripts.
Conky puts `conky_' in front of function_name to prevent accidental
calls to the wrong function unless you place `conky_' in front of it
yourself.
.TP
\f[B]machine\f[R]
Machine, e.g.\ i686, x86_64.
.TP
\f[B]mails\f[R] \f[B](mailbox) (interval)\f[R]
Mail count in the specified mailbox or your mail spool if not.
Both mbox and maildir type mailboxes are supported.
You can use a program like fetchmail to get mails from some server using
your favourite protocol.
See also new_mails.
.TP
\f[B]mboxscan\f[R] \f[B](\-n number of messages to print) (\-fw from width) (\-sw subject width) mbox\f[R]
Print a summary of recent messages in an mbox format mailbox.
mbox parameter is the filename of the mailbox (can be encapsulated using
\(cq\(lq\(cq, ie.
${mboxscan \-n 10\(rq/home/brenden/some box\(rq}
.TP
\f[B]mem\f[R]
Amount of memory in use.
.TP
\f[B]memactive\f[R]
Amount of active memory.
FreeBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]memavail\f[R]
Amount of available memory as recorded in /proc/meminfo.
Linux 3.14+ only.
.TP
\f[B]membar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Bar that shows amount of memory in use.
.TP
\f[B]memdirty\f[R]
Amount of \(lqdirty\(rq memory.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]memeasyfree\f[R]
Amount of free memory including the memory that is very easily freed
(buffers/cache).
.TP
\f[B]memfree\f[R]
Amount of free memory.
.TP
\f[B]memgauge\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Gauge that shows amount of memory in use (see cpugauge).
.TP
\f[B]memgraph\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Memory usage graph.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use the \-l
switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]meminactive\f[R]
Amount of inactive memory.
FreeBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]memlaundry\f[R]
Amount of memory in the laundry queue.
FreeBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]memmax\f[R]
Total amount of memory.
.TP
\f[B]memperc\f[R]
Percentage of memory in use.
.TP
\f[B]memwired\f[R]
Amount of wired memory.
FreeBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]memwithbuffers\f[R]
Amount of memory in use, including that used by system buffers and
caches.
.TP
\f[B]memwithbuffersbar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Bar that shows amount of memory in use (including memory used by system
buffers and caches).
.TP
\f[B]memwithbuffersgraph\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m value)\f[R]
Memory usage graph including memory used by system buffers and cache.
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use the \-l
switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]mixer\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Prints the mixer value as reported by the OS.
On Linux, this variable uses the OSS emulation, so you need the proper
kernel module loaded.
Default mixer is \(lqVol\(rq, but you can specify one of the available
OSS controls: \(lqVol\(rq, \(lqBass\(rq, \(lqTrebl\(rq, \(lqSynth\(rq,
\(lqPcm\(rq, \(lqSpkr\(rq, \(lqLine\(rq, \(lqMic\(rq, \(lqCD\(rq,
\(lqMix\(rq, \(lqPcm2\(rq, \(lqRec\(rq, \(lqIGain\(rq, \(lqOGain\(rq,
\(lqLine1\(rq, \(lqLine2\(rq, \(lqLine3\(rq, \(lqDigital1\(rq,
\(lqDigital2\(rq, \(lqDigital3\(rq, \(lqPhoneIn\(rq, \(lqPhoneOut\(rq,
\(lqVideo\(rq, \(lqRadio\(rq and \(lqMonitor\(rq.
.RS
.PP
Default: Vol
.RE
.TP
\f[B]mixerbar\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Displays mixer value in a bar as reported by the OS.
See docs for $mixer for details on arguments.
.TP
\f[B]mixerl\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Prints the left channel mixer value as reported by the OS.
See docs for $mixer for details on arguments.
.TP
\f[B]mixerlbar\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Displays the left channel mixer value in a bar as reported by the OS.
See docs for $mixer for details on arguments.
.TP
\f[B]mixerr\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Prints the right channel mixer value as reported by the OS.
See docs for $mixer for details on arguments.
.TP
\f[B]mixerrbar\f[R] \f[B](device)\f[R]
Displays the right channel mixer value in a bar as reported by the OS.
See docs for $mixer for details on arguments.
.TP
\f[B]moc_album\f[R]
Album of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_artist\f[R]
Artist of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Bar of the current MOC song\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]moc_bitrate\f[R]
Bitrate in the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_avgbitrate\f[R]
Average bitrate in the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_curtime\f[R]
Current time of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_cursec\f[R]
Current time of the current MOC song in seconds.
.TP
\f[B]moc_file\f[R]
File name of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_percent\f[R]
Percent of current MOC song\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]moc_rate\f[R]
Rate of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_song\f[R]
The current song name being played in MOC.
.TP
\f[B]moc_state\f[R]
Current state of MOC; playing, stopped etc.
.TP
\f[B]moc_timeleft\f[R]
Time left in the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_title\f[R]
Title of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_totaltime\f[R]
Total length of the current MOC song.
.TP
\f[B]moc_totalsec\f[R]
Total length of the current MOC song in seconds.
.TP
\f[B]monitor\f[R]
Number of the monitor on which conky is running or the message \(lqNot
running in X\(rq if this is the case.
.TP
\f[B]monitor_number\f[R]
Number of monitors or the message \(lqNot running in X\(rq if this is
the case.
.TP
\f[B]mouse_speed\f[R]
Display mouse speed.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_album\f[R]
Album in current MPD song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_albumartist\f[R]
Artist of the album of the current MPD song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_artist\f[R]
Artist in current MPD song must be enabled at compile.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Bar of mpd\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_bitrate\f[R]
Bitrate of current song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_comment\f[R] \f[B](max\-length)\f[R]
Comment of current MPD song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_date\f[R]
Date of current song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_elapsed\f[R]
Song\(cqs elapsed time.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_file\f[R]
Prints the file name of the current MPD song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_length\f[R]
Song\(cqs length.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_name\f[R]
Prints the MPD name field.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_percent\f[R]
Percent of song\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_random\f[R]
Random status (On/Off).
.TP
\f[B]mpd_repeat\f[R]
Repeat status (On/Off).
.TP
\f[B]mpd_smart\f[R] \f[B](max length)\f[R]
Prints the song name in either the form \(lqartist \- title\(rq or file
name, depending on whats available.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_status\f[R]
Playing, stopped, et cetera.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_title\f[R] \f[B](max length)\f[R]
Title of current MPD song.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_track\f[R]
Prints the MPD track field.
.TP
\f[B]mpd_vol\f[R]
MPD\(cqs volume.
.TP
\f[B]mysql\f[R] \f[B]query\f[R]
Shows the first field of the first row of the result of the query.
.TP
\f[B]nameserver\f[R] \f[B](index)\f[R]
Print a nameserver from /etc/resolv.conf.
.TP
\f[B]new_mails\f[R] \f[B](mailbox) (interval)\f[R]
Unread mail count in the specified mailbox or mail spool if not.
Both mbox and maildir type mailboxes are supported.
.TP
\f[B]no_update\f[R] \f[B]text\f[R]
Shows text and parses the vars in it, but doesn\(cqt update them.
Use this for things that do not change while conky is running, like
$machine, $conky_version,\&...
By not updating this you can save some resources.
.TP
\f[B]nodename\f[R]
Hostname.
.TP
\f[B]nodename_short\f[R]
Short hostname (same as `hostname \-s' shell command).
.TP
\f[B]nvidia\f[R] \f[B]argument (GPU_ID)\f[R]
Nvidia graphics card information via the XNVCtrl library or the NVML
library.
.RS
.PP
Temperatures are printed as float, all other values as integers.
.PP
\f[B]GPU_ID:\f[R] Optional parameter to choose the GPU to be used as
0,1,2,3,..
Default parameter is 0
.PP
\f[B]Possible arguments:\f[R]
.PP
.TS
