.TP
\f[B]nvidiabar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) argument (GPU_ID)\f[R]
Same as nvidia, except it draws its output in a horizontal bar.
The height and width parameters are optional, and default to the
default_bar_height and default_bar_width config settings, respectively.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]GPU_ID:\f[R] Optional parameter to choose the GPU to be used as
0,1,2,3,..
Default parameter is 0
.PP
\f[B]Note the following arguments are\f[R] incompatible:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]gputempthreshold\f[R] (\f[CR]threshold\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]gpufreqmin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]gpufreqmax\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]memfreqmin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]memfreqmax\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]mtrfreqmin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]mtrfreqmax\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]perflevelmin\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]perflevelmax\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]perfmode\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]memtotal\f[R] (\f[CR]memmax\f[R])
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]fanspeed\f[R]
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]powerlimit\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]nvidiagauge\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) argument (GPU_ID)\f[R]
Same as nvidiabar, except a round gauge (much like a vehicle
speedometer).
The height and width parameters are optional, and default to the
default_gauge_height and default_gauge_width config settings,
respectively.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]GPU_ID:\f[R] Optional parameter to choose the GPU to be used as
0,1,2,3,..
Default parameter is 0
.PP
For possible arguments see nvidia and nvidiabar.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]nvidiagraph\f[R] \f[B]argument (height),(width) (gradient color 1) (gradient color 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m) GPU_ID\f[R]
Same as nvidiabar, except a horizontally scrolling graph with values
from 0\-100 plotted on the vertical axis.
The height and width parameters are optional, and default to the
default_graph_height and default_graph_width config settings,
respectively.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]GPU_ID:\f[R] NOT optional.
This parameter allows to choose the GPU to be used as 0,1,2,3,..
.PP
For possible arguments see nvidia and nvidiabar.
To learn more about the \-t \-l \-x \-y and gradient color options, see
execgraph.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]offset\f[R] \f[B](pixels)\f[R]
Move text over by N pixels.
See also $voffset.
.TP
\f[B]outlinecolor\f[R] \f[B](color)\f[R]
Change outline color.
.TP
\f[B]pa_card_active_profile\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default card active profile.
.TP
\f[B]pa_card_name\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default card name.
.TP
\f[B]pa_sink_active_port_description\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default sink active port description.
.TP
\f[B]pa_sink_active_port_name\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default sink active port name.
.TP
\f[B]pa_sink_description\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default sink description.
.TP
\f[B]pa_sink_volume\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default sink volume percentage.
.TP
\f[B]pa_sink_volumebar\f[R]
Pulseaudio\(cqs default sink volume bar.
.TP
\f[B]password\f[R] \f[B](length)\f[R]
Generate random passwords.
.TP
\f[B]pb_battery\f[R] \f[B]item\f[R]
If running on Apple powerbook/ibook, display information on battery
status.
The item parameter specifies, what information to display.
Exactly one item must be specified.
Valid items are:
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(3.6n) lw(66.4n).
T{
Item
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]status\f[R]
T}@T{
Display if battery is fully charged, charging, discharging or absent
(running on AC)
T}
T{
\f[CR]percent\f[R]
T}@T{
Display charge of battery in percent, if charging or discharging.
Nothing will be displayed, if battery is fully charged or absent.
T}
T{
\f[CR]time\f[R]
T}@T{
Display the time remaining until the battery will be fully charged or
discharged at current rate.
Nothing is displayed, if battery is absent or if it\(cqs present but
fully charged and not discharging.
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]pid_chroot\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Directory used as rootdirectory by the process (this will be \(lq/\(rq
unless the process did a chroot syscall).
.TP
\f[B]pid_cmdline\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Command line this process was invoked with.
.TP
\f[B]pid_cwd\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Current working directory of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_egid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The effective gid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_environ\f[R] \f[B]pid varname\f[R]
Contents of a environment\-var of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_environ_list\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
List of environment\-vars that the process can see.
.TP
\f[B]pid_euid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The effective uid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_exe\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Path to executed command that started the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_fsgid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The file system gid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_fsuid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The file system uid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_gid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The real gid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_nice\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The nice value of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_openfiles\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
List of files that the process has open.
.TP
\f[B]pid_parent\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The pid of the parent of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_priority\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The priority of the process (see `priority' in \(lqman 5 proc\(rq).
.TP
\f[B]pid_read\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Total number of bytes read by the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_sgid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The saved set gid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_state\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
State of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_state_short\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
One of the chars in \(lqRSDZTW\(rq representing the state of the process
where R is running, S is sleeping in an interruptible wait, D is waiting
in uninterruptible disk sleep, Z is zombie, T is traced or stopped (on a
signal), and W is paging.
.TP
\f[B]pid_stderr\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Filedescriptor binded to the STDERR of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_stdin\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Filedescriptor binded to the STDIN of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_stdout\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Filedescriptor binded to the STDOUT of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_suid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The saved set uid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_thread_list\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
List with pid\(cqs from threads from this process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_threads\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Number of threads in process containing this thread.
.TP
\f[B]pid_time\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Sum of $pid_time_kernelmode and $pid_time_usermode.
.TP
\f[B]pid_time_kernelmode\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Amount of time that the process has been scheduled in kernel mode in
seconds.
.TP
\f[B]pid_time_usermode\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Amount of time that the process has been scheduled in user mode in
seconds.
.TP
\f[B]pid_uid\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
The real uid of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmdata\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Data segment size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmexe\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Text segment size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmhwm\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Peak resident set size (\(lqhigh water mark\(rq) of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmlck\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Locked memory size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmlib\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Shared library code size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmpeak\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Peak virtual memory size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmpte\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Page table entries size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmrss\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Resident set size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmsize\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Virtual memory size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_vmstk\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Stack segment size of the process.
.TP
\f[B]pid_write\f[R] \f[B]pid\f[R]
Total number of bytes written by the process.
.TP
\f[B]platform\f[R] \f[B](dev) type n (factor offset)\f[R]
Platform sensor from sysfs (Linux 2.6).
Parameter dev may be omitted if you have only one platform device.
Platform type is either \f[CR]in\f[R] or \f[CR]vol\f[R] meaning voltage;
\f[CR]fan\f[R] meaning fan; \f[CR]temp\f[R] meaning temperature.
Parameter n is number of the sensor.
See \f[CR]/sys/bus/platform/devices/\f[R] on your local computer.
The optional arguments \f[CR]factor\f[R] and \f[CR]offset\f[R] allow
precalculation of the raw input, which is being modified as follows:
\f[CR]input = input * factor + offset\f[R].
Note that they have to be given as decimal values (i.e.
contain at least one decimal place).
.TP
\f[B]platformbar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width) (dev) type n (factor offset)\f[R]
Same as platform, but displays the sensor reading as a horizontal bar.
Use factor and offset to scale the value reported by platform into the
0\-100 range.
Temperature values are already converted from millidegrees.
Height and width default to default_bar_height and default_bar_width
respectively.
.TP
\f[B]pop3_unseen\f[R] \f[B](args)\f[R]
Displays the number of unseen messages in your global POP3 inbox by
default.
You can define individual POP3 inboxes separately by passing arguments
to this object.
Arguments are: \(lqhost user pass [\-i interval (in seconds)] [\-p port]
[\-e `command'] [\-r retries]\(rq.
Default port is 110, default interval is 5 minutes, and default number
of retries before giving up is 5.
If the password is supplied as \(cq*\(cq, you will be prompted to enter
the password when Conky starts.
.TP
\f[B]pop3_used\f[R] \f[B](args)\f[R]
Displays the amount of space (in MiB, 2\(ha20) used in your global POP3
inbox by default.
You can define individual POP3 inboxes separately by passing arguments
to this object.
Arguments are: \(lqhost user pass [\-i interval (in seconds)] [\-p port]
[\-e `command'] [\-r retries]\(rq.
Default port is 110, default interval is 5 minutes, and default number
of retries before giving up is 5.
If the password is supplied as \(cq*\(cq, you will be prompted to enter
the password when Conky starts.
.TP
\f[B]processes\f[R]
Total processes (sleeping and running).
.TP
\f[B]read_tcp\f[R] \f[B](host) port\f[R]
Connects to a tcp port on a host (default is localhost), reads every
char available at the moment and shows them.
.TP
\f[B]read_udp\f[R] \f[B](host) port\f[R]
Connects to a udp port on a host (default is localhost), reads every
char available at the moment and shows them.
.TP
\f[B]replied_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails marked as replied in the specified mailbox or mail spool
if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]rss\f[R] \f[B]uri interval_in_seconds action (num_par (spaces_in_front))\f[R]
Download and parse RSS feeds.
The interval may be a (floating point) value greater than 0.
Action may be one of the following: feed_title, item_title (with num
par), item_desc (with num par) and item_titles (when using this action
and spaces_in_front is given conky places that many spaces in front of
each item).
This object is threaded, and once a thread is created it can\(cqt be
explicitly destroyed.
One thread will run for each URI specified.
You can use any protocol that Curl supports.
.TP
\f[B]rstrip\f[R] \f[B]text\f[R]
Strips all trailing whitespace from input.
.TP
\f[B]running_processes\f[R]
Running processes (not sleeping).
Requires Linux 2.6.
.TP
\f[B]running_threads\f[R]
Number of running (runnable) threads.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]scroll\f[R] \f[B](direction) length (step) (interval) text\f[R]
Scroll `text' by `step' characters to the left or right (set `direction'
to `left' or `right' or `wait') showing `length' number of characters at
the same time.
The text may also contain variables.
`step' is optional and defaults to 1 if not set.
`direction' is optional and defaults to left if not set.
When direction is `wait' then text will scroll left and wait for
`interval' itertations at the beginning and end of the text.
If a var creates output on multiple lines then the lines are placed
behind each other separated with a `|'\-sign.
If you change the textcolor inside $scroll it will automatically have
it\(cqs old value back at the end of $scroll.
The end and the start of text will be separated by `length' number of
spaces unless direction is `wait'.
.TP
\f[B]seen_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails marked as seen in the specified mailbox or mail spool if
not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]shadecolor\f[R] \f[B](color)\f[R]
Change shading color.
.TP
\f[B]shmem\f[R]
Amount of shared memory.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]sip_status\f[R] \f[B](switch)\f[R]
Prints info regarding System Integrity Protection (SIP) on macOS.
If no switch is provided, prints SIP status (enabled / disabled), else,
status of the specific SIP feature corresponding to the switch provided.
.RS
.PP
Below are shown the available switches:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l l.
T{
SWITCH
T}@T{
RESULT
T}@T{
STATUS
T}
_
T{
0
T}@T{
apple internal
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
1
T}@T{
forbid untrusted kexts
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
2
T}@T{
forbid task\-for\-pid
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
3
T}@T{
restrict filesystem
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
4
T}@T{
forbid kernel\-debugger
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
5
T}@T{
restrict dtrace
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
6
T}@T{
restrict nvram
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
7
T}@T{
forbid device\-configuration
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
8
T}@T{
forbid any\-recovery\-os
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
T{
9
T}@T{
forbid user\-approved\-kexts
T}@T{
YES/NO
T}
.TE
.PP
uses unsupported configuration?: If yes, prints \(lqunsupported
configuration, beware!\(rq Else, prints \(lqconfiguration is ok\(rq.
.PP
USAGE:
.IP
.EX
$ conky \-t \(aq${sip_status}\(aq
# print SIP status
$ conky \-t \(aq${sip_status 0}\(aq
# print allows apple\-internal? Yes or No?
.EE
.PP
NOTES:
.IP \(bu 2
Available for all macOS versions (even the ones prior El Capitan where
SIP was first introduced)
.IP \(bu 2
If run on versions prior El Capitan SIP is unavailable, so all you will
get is \(lqunsupported\(rq.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]smapi\f[R] \f[B](ARGS)\f[R]
When using smapi, display contents of the
\f[CR]/sys/devices/platform/smapi\f[R] directory.
ARGS are either \f[CR](FILENAME)\f[R] or
\f[CR]bat (INDEX) (FILENAME)\f[R] to display the corresponding files\(cq
content.
This is a very raw method of accessing the smapi values.
When available, better use one of the smapi_* variables instead.
.TP
\f[B]smapi_bat_bar\f[R] \f[B](INDEX),(height),(width)\f[R]
when using smapi, display the remaining capacity of the battery with
index INDEX as a bar.
.TP
\f[B]smapi_bat_perc\f[R] \f[B](INDEX)\f[R]
when using smapi, display the remaining capacity in percent of the
battery with index INDEX.
This is a separate variable because it supports the `use_spacer'
configuration option.
.TP
\f[B]smapi_bat_power\f[R] \f[B]INDEX\f[R]
when using smapi, display the current power of the battery with index
INDEX in watt.
This is a separate variable because the original read out value is being
converted from mW.
The sign of the output reflects charging (positive) or discharging
(negative) state.
.TP
\f[B]smapi_bat_temp\f[R] \f[B]INDEX\f[R]
when using smapi, display the current temperature of the battery with
index INDEX in degree Celsius.
This is a separate variable because the original read out value is being
converted from milli degree Celsius.
.TP
\f[B]sony_fanspeed\f[R]
Displays the Sony VAIO fanspeed information if sony\-laptop kernel
support is enabled.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]startcase\f[R] \f[B]text\f[R]
Capitalises the start of each word.
.TP
\f[B]stippled_hr\f[R] \f[B](space) (height) (width)\f[R]
Stippled (dashed) horizontal line.
.TP
