.TP
\f[B]swap\f[R]
Amount of swap in use.
.TP
\f[B]swapbar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Bar that shows amount of swap in use.
.TP
\f[B]swapfree\f[R]
Amount of free swap.
.TP
\f[B]swapmax\f[R]
Total amount of swap.
.TP
\f[B]swapperc\f[R]
Percentage of swap in use.
.TP
\f[B]sysctlbyname\f[R] \f[B](name)\f[R]
Print sysctl value by name.
FreeBSD only.
.TP
\f[B]sysname\f[R]
System name, e.g.\ Linux.
.TP
\f[B]tab\f[R] \f[B](width, (start))\f[R]
Puts a tab of the specified width, starting from column `start'.
The unit is pixels for both arguments.
.TP
\f[B]tail\f[R] \f[B]logfile lines (next_check)\f[R]
Displays last N lines of supplied text file.
The file is checked every `next_check' update.
If next_check is not supplied, Conky defaults to 2.
Max of 30 lines can be displayed, or until the text buffer is filled.
.TP
\f[B]tcp_ping\f[R] \f[B]host (port)\f[R]
Displays the number of microseconds it takes to get a reply on a ping to
to tcp `port' on `host'.
`port' is optional and has 80 as default.
This works on both open and closed ports, just make sure that the port
is not behind a firewall or you will get `down' as answer.
It\(cqs best to test a closed port instead of an open port, you will get
a quicker response.
.TP
\f[B]tcp_portmon\f[R] \f[B]port_begin port_end item (index)\f[R]
TCP port (both IPv6 and IPv4) monitor for specified local ports.
Port numbers must be in the range 1 to 65535.
Valid items are:
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Item
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]count\f[R]
T}@T{
Total number of connections in the range
T}
T{
\f[CR]rip\f[R]
T}@T{
Remote ip address
T}
T{
\f[CR]rhost\f[R]
T}@T{
Remote host name
T}
T{
\f[CR]rport\f[R]
T}@T{
Remote port number
T}
T{
\f[CR]rservice\f[R]
T}@T{
Remote service name from \f[CR]/etc/services\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]lip\f[R]
T}@T{
Local ip address
T}
T{
\f[CR]lhost\f[R]
T}@T{
Local host name
T}
T{
\f[CR]lport\f[R]
T}@T{
Local port number
T}
T{
\f[CR]lservice\f[R]
T}@T{
Local service name from \f[CR]/etc/services\f[R]
T}
.TE
.PP
The connection index provides you with access to each connection in the
port monitor.
The monitor will return information for index values from 0 to n\-1
connections.
Values higher than n\-1 are simply ignored.
For the \f[CR]count\f[R] item, the connection index must be omitted.
It is required for all other items.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(21.3n) lw(48.7n).
T{
Example
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]${tcp_portmon 6881 6999 count}\f[R]
T}@T{
Displays the number of connections in the bittorrent port range
T}
T{
\f[CR]${tcp_portmon 22 22 rip 0}\f[R]
T}@T{
Displays the remote host ip of the first sshd connection
T}
T{
\f[CR]${tcp_portmon 22 22 rip 9}\f[R]
T}@T{
Displays the remote host ip of the tenth sshd connection
T}
T{
\f[CR]${tcp_portmon 1 1024 rhost 0}\f[R]
T}@T{
Displays the remote host name of the first connection on a privileged
port
T}
T{
\f[CR]${tcp_portmon 1 1024 rport 4}\f[R]
T}@T{
Displays the remote host port of the fifth connection on a privileged
port
T}
T{
\f[CR]${tcp_portmon 1 65535 lservice 14}\f[R]
T}@T{
Displays the local service name of the fifteenth connection in the range
of all ports
T}
.TE
.PP
Note that port monitor variables which share the same port range
actually refer to the same monitor, so many references to a single port
range for different items and different indexes all use the same monitor
internally.
In other words, the program avoids creating redundant monitors.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]templateN\f[R] \f[B](arg1) (arg2) (arg3 \&...)\f[R]
Evaluate the content of the templateN configuration variable (where N is
a value between 0 and 9, inclusively), applying substitutions as
described in the documentation of the corresponding configuration
variable.
The number of arguments is optional, but must match the highest referred
index in the template.
You can use the same special sequences in each argument as the ones
valid for a template definition, e.g.\ to allow an argument to contain a
whitespace.
Also simple nesting of templates is possible this way.
.RS
.PP
Here are some examples of template definitions, note they are placed
between \f[CR][[ ... ]]\f[R] instead of \f[CR]...\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
template0 = [[$12]]
template1 = [[1: ${fs_used 2} / ${fs_size 2}]]
template2 = [[1 2]]
.EE
.PP
The following list shows sample usage of the templates defined above,
with the equivalent syntax when not using any template at all:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(36.2n) lw(33.8n).
T{
Using template
T}@T{
Same without template
T}
_
T{
\f[CR]${template0 node name}\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]$nodename\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]${template1 root /}\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]root: ${fs_free /} / ${fs_size /}\f[R]
T}
T{
\f[CR]${template1 ${template2 disk root} /}\f[R]
T}@T{
\f[CR]disk root: ${fs_free /} / ${fs_size /}\f[R]
T}
T{
\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
T}@T{
\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em\(em
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]texeci\f[R] \f[B]interval command\f[R]
Runs a command at an interval inside a thread and displays the output.
Same as $execi, except the command is run inside a thread.
Use this if you have a slow script to keep Conky updating.
You should make the interval slightly longer than the time it takes your
script to execute.
For example, if you have a script that take 5 seconds to execute, you
should make the interval at least 6 seconds.
See also $execi.
This object will clean up the thread when it is destroyed, so it can
safely be used in a nested fashion, though it may not produce the
desired behaviour if used this way.
.TP
\f[B]texecpi\f[R] \f[B]interval command\f[R]
Same as execpi, except the command is run inside a thread.
.TP
\f[B]threads\f[R]
Total threads.
.TP
\f[B]time\f[R] \f[B](format)\f[R]
Local time, see \(lqman strftime\(rq to get more information about
format.
.TP
\f[B]to_bytes\f[R] \f[B]size\f[R]
If `size' is a number followed by a size\-unit (kilobyte,mb,GiB,\&...)
then it converts the size to bytes and shows it without unit, otherwise
it just shows `size'.
.TP
\f[B]top\f[R] \f[B]type num\f[R]
This takes arguments in the form:top (name) (number) Basically,
processes are ranked from highest to lowest in terms of cpu usage, which
is what (num) represents.
The types are: \(lqname\(rq, \(lqpid\(rq, \(lqcpu\(rq, \(lqmem\(rq,
\(lqmem_res\(rq, \(lqmem_vsize\(rq, \(lqtime\(rq, \(lquid\(rq,
\(lquser\(rq, \(lqio_perc\(rq, \(lqio_read\(rq and \(lqio_write\(rq.
There can be a max of 10 processes listed.
.TP
\f[B]top_io\f[R] \f[B]type num\f[R]
Same as top, except sorted by the amount of I/O the process has done
during the update interval.
.TP
\f[B]top_mem\f[R] \f[B]type num\f[R]
Same as top, except sorted by mem usage instead of cpu.
.TP
\f[B]top_time\f[R] \f[B]type num\f[R]
Same as top, except sorted by total CPU time instead of current CPU
usage.
.TP
\f[B]totaldown\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Total download, overflows at 4 GB on Linux with 32\-bit arch and there
doesn\(cqt seem to be a way to know how many times it has already done
that before conky has started.
.TP
\f[B]totalup\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Total upload, this one too, may overflow.
.TP
\f[B]trashed_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails marked as trashed in the specified mailbox or mail spool
if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]tztime\f[R] \f[B](timezone (format))\f[R]
Local time for specified timezone, see man strftime to get more
information about format.
The timezone argument is specified in similar fashion as TZ environment
variable.
For hints, look in /usr/share/zoneinfo.
e.g.\ US/Pacific, Europe/Zurich, etc.
.TP
\f[B]uid_name\f[R] \f[B]uid\f[R]
Username of user with this uid.
.TP
\f[B]unflagged_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails not marked as flagged in the specified mailbox or mail
spool if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]unforwarded_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails not marked as forwarded in the specified mailbox or mail
spool if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]unreplied_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of mails not marked as replied in the specified mailbox or mail
spool if not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]unseen_mails\f[R] \f[B](maildir) (interval)\f[R]
Number of new or unseen mails in the specified mailbox or mail spool if
not.
Only maildir type mailboxes are supported, mbox type will return \-1.
.TP
\f[B]updates\f[R] \f[B]Number of updates\f[R]
for debugging.
.TP
\f[B]uppercase\f[R] \f[B]text\f[R]
Converts all letters into uppercase.
.TP
\f[B]upspeed\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Upload speed in suitable IEC units.
.TP
\f[B]upspeedf\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Upload speed in KiB with one decimal.
.TP
\f[B]upspeedgraph\f[R] \f[B](netdev) (height),(width) (gradient colour 1) (gradient colour 2) (scale) (\-t) (\-l) (\-x) (\-y) (\-m)\f[R]
Upload speed graph, colours defined in hex, minus the #.
If scale is non\-zero, it defines the maximum value of the graph (in
bytes per second).
Uses a logarithmic scale (to see small numbers) when you use the \-l
switch.
Takes the switch `\-t' to use a temperature gradient, which makes the
gradient values change depending on the amplitude of a particular graph
value (try it and see).
The flag `\-x' inverts the x axis and `\-y' inverts the y axis of the
graph.
The flag `\-m' sets a nonzero minimum/lowerbound, ensuring that all
values are at least the specified minimum (excluding zero).
.TP
\f[B]uptime\f[R]
Uptime.
.TP
\f[B]uptime_short\f[R]
Uptime in a shorter format.
.TP
\f[B]user_names\f[R]
Lists the names of the users logged in.
.TP
\f[B]user_number\f[R]
Number of users logged in.
.TP
\f[B]user_terms\f[R]
Lists the consoles in use.
.TP
\f[B]user_time\f[R] \f[B]console\f[R]
Lists how long the user for the given console has been logged in for.
.TP
\f[B]user_times\f[R]
Lists how long users have been logged in for.
.TP
\f[B]utime\f[R] \f[B](format)\f[R]
Display time in UTC (universal coordinate time).
.TP
\f[B]v6addrs\f[R] \f[B](\-n) (\-s) (interface)\f[R]
IPv6 addresses for an interface, followed by netmask if \-n is specified
and scope with \-s.
Scopes are Global(G), Host\-local(H), Link\-local(L), Site\-local(S),
Compat(C) and Unspecified(/).
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]version\f[R]
Git version number.
DragonFly only.
.TP
\f[B]voffset\f[R] \f[B](pixels)\f[R]
Change vertical offset by N pixels.
Negative values will cause text to overlap.
See also $offset.
.TP
\f[B]voltage_mv\f[R] \f[B](n)\f[R]
Returns CPU #n\(cqs voltage in mV.
CPUs are counted from 1.
.RS
.PP
Default: 1
.RE
.TP
\f[B]voltage_v\f[R] \f[B](n)\f[R]
Returns CPU #n\(cqs voltage in V. CPUs are counted from 1.
.RS
.PP
Default: 1
.RE
.TP
\f[B]wireless_ap\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless access point MAC address.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_bitrate\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless bitrate (ie 11 Mb/s).
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_channel\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
WLAN channel on which device `net' is listening.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_essid\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless access point ESSID.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_freq\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Frequency on which device `net' is listening.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_link_bar\f[R] \f[B](net) (height),(width)\f[R]
Wireless link quality bar.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_link_qual\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless link quality.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_link_qual_max\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless link quality maximum value.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_link_qual_perc\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless link quality in percents.
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]wireless_mode\f[R] \f[B](net)\f[R]
Wireless mode (Managed/Ad\-Hoc/Master).
Linux only.
.TP
\f[B]words\f[R] \f[B]textfile\f[R]
Displays the number of words in the given file.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_album\f[R]
Album in current XMMS2 song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_artist\f[R]
Artist in current XMMS2 song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_bar\f[R] \f[B](height),(width)\f[R]
Bar of XMMS2\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_bitrate\f[R]
Bitrate of current song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_comment\f[R]
Comment in current XMMS2 song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_date\f[R]
Returns song\(cqs date.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_duration\f[R]
Duration of current song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_elapsed\f[R]
Song\(cqs elapsed time.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_genre\f[R]
Genre in current XMMS2 song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_id\f[R]
XMMS2 id of current song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_percent\f[R]
Percent of song\(cqs progress.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_playlist\f[R]
Returns the XMMS2 playlist.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_size\f[R]
Size of current song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_smart\f[R]
Prints the song name in either the form \(lqartist \- title\(rq or file
name, depending on whats available.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_status\f[R]
XMMS2 status (Playing, Paused, Stopped, or Disconnected).
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_timesplayed\f[R]
Number of times a song was played (presumably).
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_title\f[R]
Title in current XMMS2 song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_tracknr\f[R]
Track number in current XMMS2 song.
.TP
\f[B]xmms2_url\f[R]
Full path to current song.
.SH LUA API
Conky features a Lua programming API and also ships with Lua bindings
for some useful libraries.
Note that the bindings require tolua++.
.PP
To use Lua with Conky, first make sure you have a version of Conky with
Lua support enabled (\f[CR]conky \-v\f[R] will report this).
Conky defines certain global functions and variables that can be
accessed from Lua code running inside Conky.
Scripts must first be loaded using the \f[CR]lua_load\f[R] configuration
option.
You can then call Lua functions through Conky\(cqs \f[CR]$lua\f[R],
\f[CR]$lua_read\f[R], and Lua hooks.
.PP
Be careful when creating threaded objects through the Lua API.
You could end up with a large number of threads running if a thread is
created with each iteration.
.PP
Note: to accommodate certain features in the Cairo library\(cqs API,
Conky will export a few additional functions for the creation of certain
structures.
These are documented below.
.TP
\f[B]RsvgDimensionData:create()\f[R]
Call this method to return a new RsvgDimensionData structure.
A creation function for this structure is not provided by the Rsvg API.
.RS
.PP
After calling this, you should use \f[CR]tolua.takeownership(rect)\f[R]
on the return value to ensure ownership is passed properly.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RsvgDimensionData:destroy()\f[R]
Call this method to free memory allocated by
\f[CR]RsvgDimensionData:create\f[R].
.RS
.PP
You should call \f[CR]tolua.releaseownership(dd)\f[R] before calling
this function to avoid double\-frees, but only if you previously called
\f[CR]tolua.takeownership(dd)\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RsvgDimensionData:get()\f[R]
Gets the values of an existing RsvgDimensionData.
.TP
\f[B]RsvgDimensionData:set(x, y, width, height)\f[R]
Sets the values of an existing RsvgDimensionData.
.TP
\f[B]RsvgRectangle:create()\f[R]
Call this method to return a new RsvgRectangle structure.
A creation function for this structure is not provided by the Rsvg API.
.RS
.PP
After calling this, you should use \f[CR]tolua.takeownership(rect)\f[R]
on the return value to ensure ownership is passed properly.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RsvgRectangle:destroy()\f[R]
Call this method to free memory allocated by
\f[CR]RsvgRectangle:create\f[R].
.RS
.PP
You should call \f[CR]tolua.releaseownership(rect)\f[R] before calling
this function to avoid double\-frees, but only if you previously called
\f[CR]tolua.takeownership(rect)\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]RsvgRectangle:get()\f[R]
Gets the values of an existing RsvgRectangle.
.TP
\f[B]RsvgRectangle:set(x, y, width, height)\f[R]
Sets the values of an existing RsvgRectangle.
.TP
\f[B]cairo_font_extents_t:create()\f[R]
Call this function to return a new cairo_font_extents_t structure.
A creation function for this structure is not provided by the cairo API.
.RS
.PP
After calling this, you should use \f[CR]tolua.takeownership(cfe)\f[R]
on the return value to ensure ownership is passed properly.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_font_extents_t:destroy(structure)\f[R]
Call this function to free memory allocated by
cairo_font_extents_t:create.
.RS
.PP
You should call \f[CR]tolua.releaseownership(cfe)\f[R] before calling
this function to avoid double\-frees, but only if you previously called
\f[CR]tolua.takeownership(cfe)\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_matrix_t:create()\f[R]
Call this function to return a new cairo_matrix_t structure.
A creation function for this structure is not provided by the cairo API.
.RS
.PP
After calling this, you should use \f[CR]tolua.takeownership(cm)\f[R] on
the return value to ensure ownership is passed properly.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_matrix_t:destroy(structure)\f[R]
Call this function to free memory allocated by cairo_matrix_t:create.
.RS
.PP
You should call \f[CR]tolua.releaseownership(cm)\f[R] before calling
this function to avoid double\-frees, but only if you previously called
\f[CR]tolua.takeownership(cm)\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_place_image(file, cr, x, y, width, height, alpha)\f[R]
Renders an image onto a cairo_t, using imlib2.
In some cases using a cairo_t and exact coordinates is more useful.
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(13.7n) lw(56.3n).
T{
Argument
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
file
T}@T{
Path to the image to render.
T}
T{
cr
T}@T{
The \f[CR]cairo_t\f[R] to render to.
T}
T{
x,y
T}@T{
Position to render the image.
T}
T{
width, height
T}@T{
The width and height to draw the image
T}
T{
alpha
T}@T{
The transparency of the image 1.0 is solid 0.0 is fully translucent.
T}
.TE
.PP
require(`cairo_imlib2_helper') in your lua file.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_text_extents_t:create()\f[R]
Call this function to return a new cairo_text_extents_t structure.
A creation function for this structure is not provided by the cairo API.
.RS
.PP
After calling this, you should use \f[CR]tolua.takeownership(cte)\f[R]
on the return value to ensure ownership is passed properly.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_text_extents_t:destroy(structure)\f[R]
Call this function to free memory allocated by
cairo_text_extents_t:create.
.RS
.PP
You should call \f[CR]tolua.releaseownership(cte)\f[R] before calling
this function to avoid double\-frees, but only if you previously called
\f[CR]tolua.takeownership(cte)\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]conky_build_arch\f[R]
A string containing the build architecture for this Conky instance.
.TP
\f[B]conky_build_info\f[R]
A string containing build information for this Conky instance, including
the version, build date, and architecture.
.TP
\f[B]conky_config\f[R]
A string containing the path of the current Conky configuration file.
.TP
\f[B]conky_info\f[R]
This table contains some information about Conky\(cqs internal data.
The following table describes the values contained:
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
T{
Key
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
update_interval
T}@T{
Conky\(cqs update interval (in seconds).
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]conky_parse(string)\f[R]
This function takes a string that is evaluated as per Conky\(cqs TEXT
section, and then returns a string with the result.
.TP
\f[B]conky_set_update_interval(number)\f[R]
Sets Conky\(cqs update interval (in seconds) to `number'.
.TP
\f[B]conky_surface()\f[R]
Returns the current \f[CR]cairo_surface_t\f[R] for the active display
output, or \f[CR]nil\f[R] if no graphical surface is available.
This is the preferred, backend\-agnostic way to obtain a drawing surface
for use with Cairo.
.RS
.PP
The returned surface is only valid for the current draw cycle.
Do not cache it across frames; call \f[CR]conky_surface()\f[R] each time
your draw hook is invoked.
.PP
Example usage:
.IP
.EX
require(\(aqcairo\(aq)
\f[B]function\f[R] conky_draw()
  \f[B]local\f[R] surface = conky_surface()
  \f[B]if\f[R] \f[B]not\f[R] surface \f[B]then\f[R] \f[B]return\f[R] \f[B]end\f[R]
  \f[B]local\f[R] cr = cairo_create(surface)
  \f[I]\-\- draw ...\f[R]
  cairo_destroy(cr)
\f[B]end\f[R]
.EE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]conky_version\f[R]
A string containing the version of the current Conky instance.
.TP
\f[B]conky_window\f[R]
This table contains read\-only information about Conky\(cqs window.
The following table describes the values contained:
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(14.4n) lw(55.6n).
T{
Key
T}@T{
Value
T}
_
T{
drawable
T}@T{
\f[B]Deprecated.\f[R] Window\(cqs drawable (Xlib Drawable).
Use \f[CR]conky_surface()\f[R] instead.
T}
T{
visual
T}@T{
\f[B]Deprecated.\f[R] Window\(cqs visual (Xlib Visual).
Use \f[CR]conky_surface()\f[R] instead.
T}
T{
display
T}@T{
\f[B]Deprecated.\f[R] Window\(cqs display (Xlib Display).
Use \f[CR]conky_surface()\f[R] instead.
T}
T{
width
T}@T{
Window width in layout points (pt; logical).
T}
T{
height
T}@T{
Window height in layout points (pt; logical).
T}
T{
pixel_size.x
T}@T{
Window width in device pixels (px; physical).
T}
T{
pixel_size.y
T}@T{
Window height in device pixels (px; physical).
T}
T{
scale
T}@T{
Scaling factor of the buffer (dpi; pixel_size / size).
T}
T{
border_inner_margin
T}@T{
Window\(cqs inner border margin (in pixels).
T}
T{
border_outer_margin
T}@T{
Window\(cqs outer border margin (in pixels).
T}
T{
border_width
T}@T{
Window\(cqs border width (in pixels).
T}
T{
text_start_x
T}@T{
The x component of the starting coordinate of text drawing.
T}
T{
text_start_y
T}@T{
The y component of the starting coordinate of text drawing.
T}
T{
text_width
T}@T{
The width of the text drawing region.
T}
T{
text_height
T}@T{
The height of the text drawing region.
T}
.TE
.PP
NOTE: This table is only defined when a GUI output is active.
The \f[CR]drawable\f[R], \f[CR]visual\f[R], and \f[CR]display\f[R]
fields are X11\-specific and deprecated.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]ret_scale_x,ret_scale_y:cairo_draw_image(file, cs, x, y, scale_x, scale_y)\f[R]
Renders an image onto a cairo_surface_t, using imlib2.
Returns the amount the image was scaled by
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(13.7n) lw(56.3n).
T{
Argument
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
file
T}@T{
Path to the image to render.
T}
T{
cs
T}@T{
The \f[CR]cairo_surface_t\f[R] to render to.
T}
T{
x,y
T}@T{
Position to render the image.
T}
T{
scale_x, scale_y
T}@T{
The amount to scale the image, 1.0 provides no scaling, 2.0 for twice
the size and
T}
T{
T}@T{
0.5 for half size etc.
Default value: No Scaling (1.0,1.0)
T}
.TE
.PP
require(`cairo_imlib2_helper') in your lua file.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_text_hp_show(cr, x, y, text, font, font_size, alignment, language, script, direction)\f[R]
Renders text to a \f[CR]cairo_t\f[R] using HarfBuzz and FreeType.
This provides significantly better text rendering than Cairo\(cqs
built\-in functions.
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(13.7n) lw(56.3n).
T{
Argument
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
cr
T}@T{
The \f[CR]cairo_t\f[R] to render to.
T}
T{
x,y
T}@T{
Position to render the text.
T}
T{
text
T}@T{
The text to render.
T}
T{
font
T}@T{
The name of the font to be used, \f[CR]Fontconfig\f[R] is used to search
for the font.
T}
T{
font_size
T}@T{
The font size.
T}
T{
alignment
T}@T{
One of \f[CR]CAIRO_TEXT_ALIGN_LEFT\f[R],
\f[CR]CAIRO_TEXT_ALIGN_RIGHT\f[R],
\f[CR]CAIRO_TEXT_ALIGN_CENTER\f[R].Default value:
\f[CR]CAIRO_TEXT_ALIGN_LEFT\f[R]
T}
T{
language
T}@T{
A string containing a BCP 47 language tag.Default value: \f[CR]en\f[R]
T}
T{
script
T}@T{
A string containing a ISO 15924 script tag.Default value: auto\-detect
from text
T}
T{
direction
T}@T{
A string representing text direction eg \f[CR]LTR\f[R], \f[CR]RTL\f[R]
or \f[CR]TTB\f[R]Default value: auto\-detect from text otherwise
\f[CR]LTR\f[R]
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]width,height:cairo_text_hp_text_size(text, font, font_size, language, script, direction)\f[R]
Used to calculate how many pixels will be required to render a string
with \f[CR]cairo_text_hp_show\f[R] returns a pair of int\(cqs with
\f[CR]width\f[R] and \f[CR]height\f[R].
.RS
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(13.7n) lw(56.3n).
T{
Argument
T}@T{
Description
T}
_
T{
cr
T}@T{
The \f[CR]cairo_t\f[R] to render to.
T}
T{
text
T}@T{
The text to render.
T}
T{
font
T}@T{
The name of the font to be used, \f[CR]Fontconfig\f[R] is used to search
for the font.
T}
T{
font_size
T}@T{
The font size.
T}
T{
language
T}@T{
A string containing a BCP 47 language tag.Default value: \f[CR]en\f[R]
T}
T{
script
T}@T{
A string containing a ISO 15924 script tag.Default value: auto\-detect
from text
T}
T{
direction
T}@T{
A string representing text direction eg \f[CR]LTR\f[R], \f[CR]RTL\f[R]
or \f[CR]TTB\f[R] Default value: auto\-detect from text otherwise
\f[CR]LTR\f[R]
T}
.TE
.RE
.TP
\f[B]cairo_text_hp_delete_fonts()\f[R]
\f[CR]cairo_text_hp_show\f[R] and \f[CR]cairo_text_hp_text_size\f[R]
both cache internal font details, this function clears those caches.
.SH "مثال‌ها (EXAMPLES)"
.TP
\f[B]conky \-t \(aq${time %D %H:%M}\(aq \-o \-u 30\f[R]
Start Conky in its own window with date and clock as text and 30 sec
update interval.
.TP
\f[B]conky \-a top_left \-x 5 \-y 500 \-d\f[R]
Start Conky to background at coordinates (5, 500).
.TP
\f[B]conky \-C > \(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R]
Do not start Conky, but have it output the builtin default config file
to \f[I]\(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R] for later customising.
.SH "فایل‌ها (FILES)"
.TP
\f[I]${sysconfdir}/conky/conky.conf\f[R]
Default system\-wide configuration file.
The value of ${sysconfdir} depends on the compile\-time options (most
likely /etc).
.TP
\f[I]\(ti/.config/conky/conky.conf\f[R]
Default personal configuration file.
.SH "اشکالات (BUGS)"
Drawing to root or some other desktop window directly doesn\(aqt work
with all window managers.
Especially doesn\(aqt work well with Gnome and it has been reported that
it doesn\(aqt work with KDE either.
Nautilus can be disabled from drawing to desktop with program
gconf\-editor.
Uncheck show_desktop in /apps/nautilus/preferences/.
There is \-w switch in Conky to set some specific window id.
You might find xwininfo \-tree useful to find the window to draw to.
You can also use \-o argument which makes Conky to create its own
window.
If you do try running Conky in its own window, be sure to read up on the
own_window_type settings and experiment.
